\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Firms Ahead Of Payrolls; Greece Concerns Build\b0\par\par\uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2012\par 13:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Dollar firmer ahead of US payrolls data\par \par \uc2 --Concerns over Greece build, weighing on sentiment to the euro\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  again\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Clare Connaghan \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was firmer across the board ahead of a U.S. employment update Friday, rising to a three-week high against the euro, as new worries about Greece and weak data from the 17-country euro region dampened sentiment towards \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  perceived as risky.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro hit $1.2865 against the dollar in European trading hours after data showed manufacturing activity shrank for the 15th straight month in October. That indicates the overall euro-zone economy deteriorated at the start of the fourth quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock markets also fell, while yields were steady on bonds issued by fiscally-stressed European governments like Italy and Spain.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The euro weakness against the dollar appears driven by positioning and squaring up ahead of payrolls, though market participants are now paying more attention to the risks surrounding the Greek parliamentary vote next week," said Elsa Lignos, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greek lawmakers are expected to vote next week on a 13.5 billion euro ($17.5 billion) austerity package that international creditors have demanded in exchange for aid. A vote on the government's 2013 spending plan is likely to follow a few days later. Analysts expect the votes to be close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aside from Greece, Spain also continues to worry the market with data showing Spanish manufacturing activity declined faster in October and Spanish car registrations fell 21.7% on the year in October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Everyone in the market keeps pushing back expectations for when Spain will ask (for financial assistance), until they are tested....there is no real political impetus for them to do so," said Adam Myers, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are generally less optimistic about European situation right now, said Mr. Myers, and that is weighing on the euro and helping the dollar in European trading. The greenback is also stronger against the yen and commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Canada, New Zealand and Australia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, attention is immediately focused on U.S. non-farm payrolls for October to be released at 1230 GMT. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires expect an increase of 125,000 jobs, compared with 114,000 in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the pound bounced off session lows against the dollar and rose to a one-month high against the euro after data showed the U.K.'s construction sector returned to muted growth in October. Data provider Markit and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chartered Institute of Purchasing and Supply\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the purchasing managers index for the construction sector in October was 50.9, up from 49.5 in September. By 1147 GMT, the pound was trading at $1.6090 against the dollar, compared with $1.6129 late Thursday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In other news, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold another 5.038 billion Hong Kong dollars (US$650 million) during European trade Friday to defend the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, according to market data. The HKMA's latest move was triggered by the U.S. dollar hitting HK$7.7500, the strong side of the Hong Kong dollar's trading band, as a flood of capital inflows amid global central banks' stimulus measures continued to strengthen Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including the Hong Kong dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, China's yuan fell against the U.S. dollar after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guided the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weaker against the greenback via its daily reference rate, although the yuan still hit its daily upper limit for the fourth consecutive day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In emerging markets, the Czech koruna continued to trade at three-month lows against the euro a day after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut all its interest rates to historic lows in an effort to boost an economy in recession since late 2011. With its base interest rate practically at zero, the bank has said it may deploy other measures to weaken the koruna and help local exporters make their goods cheaper abroad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1147 GMT, the euro was trading at $1.2882 against the dollar, compared with $1.2943, late Thursday in New York, according to trading system EBS. The dollar was at Y80.25 against the yen, compared with Y80.13, while the euro was at Y103.38, compared with Y103.73.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 70.253 from 70.017.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A summary of key levels for chart-watching technical strategists is below:\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/USD    USD/JPY    GBP/USD    USD/CHF \par  \par Spot 1135 GMT     1.2882     80.25      1.6088     0.9370 \par 3 Day Trend       Bearish    Bullish    Range      Bullish \par Weekly Trend      Range      Bullish    Range      Range \par 200 day ma        1.2878     79.11      1.5877     0.9391 \par 3rd Resistance    1.2983     80.83      1.6237     0.9455 \par 2nd Resistance    1.2954     80.63      1.6178     0.9438 \par 1st Resistance    1.2925     80.38      1.6144     0.9387 \par Pivot*            1.2950     80.04      1.6142     0.9319 \par 1st Support       1.2865     80.11      1.6069     0.9325 \par 2nd Support       1.2835     79.92      1.6038     0.9310 \par 3rd Support       1.2803     79.69      1.6007     0.9296 \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       USD Index \par  \par Spot 1135 GMT     80.350 \par 3 Day Trend       Bullish \par Weekly Trend      Bullish \par 200 day ma        80.388 \par 3rd Resistance    80.963 \par 2nd Resistance    80.539 \par 1st Resistance    80.389 \par Pivot*            80.000 \par 1st Support       80.138 \par 2nd Support       80.062 \par 3rd Support       79.985 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Dow Jones Technical Strategist Francis Bray contributed to this story.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Clare Connaghan at clare.connaghan@dowjones.com [ 11-02-12 0839ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020121102e8b20002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
