\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Up, Hong Kong Hits 15-Month High\b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2012\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Asian markets rise on positive U.S. economic data\par \par \uc2 -- Hang Seng Index hits 15-month high above 22,000\par \par \uc2 -- Investors await near-term events including U.S. jobs data and the U.S. Presidential election\par \par \uc2 Asian markets were higher on Friday, as positive U.S. economic data released Thursday contributed to growing optimism over the global economy, pushing Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index to its highest level since August 2011.\par \par \uc2 Regional stocks took their cue from Wall Street, where markets started November on an upbeat note following positive economic data. At the forefront was the Institute of Supply Management's reading on manufacturing, which showed a slightly better-than-expected expansion in October.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. factory numbers followed Chinese manufacturing data, released early Thursday, which was taken as a sign that Asia's largest economy is recovering from its recent slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The next dose of economic data from the U.S. will be the government's employment report due later Friday. After that, the world's two largest economies will decide on their new leaders, with the U.S. Presidential election and the start of China's party congress both taking place next week.\par \par \uc2 The strong economic data helped the Hang Seng Index climb above the 20,000 mark for the first time in fifteen months. The index was up 1.3% at 22,099.82, benefiting from increased confidence in the Chinese economy as well as liquidity inflows into the city, as evidenced by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 "There are four stages in a stock market cycle, including hope, optimism, worry and disappointment. The Hong Kong market is in the hope stage," said Edward Huang, strategist at Haitong International in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Big movers in Hong Kong included Sands China, which was up 4.8%; food company \uc2 Want Want China Holdings\uc2 , which was 4.7% higher; and retailer \uc2 Esprit Holdings\uc2 , which gained 3%.\par \par \uc2 In mainland China, the Shanghai Composite was down 0.3%, moderating after jumping 1.7% on Thursday following the most recent Chinese manufacturing data.\par \par \uc2 The increase in risk appetite was also evident in the movement in the yen, which weakened against the U.S. dollar. The U.S. dollar was at Y80.24 Friday, extending its 0.4% overnight rise against the yen.\par \par \uc2 The weaker yen was a boon for Japanese stocks, with the Nikkei up 1.1%, while major electronics companies responded to earnings reports.\par \par \uc2 Sharp fell 4.1% after posting a loss for the latest quarter and predicting a second straight year of record deficits; while \uc2 Sony\uc2  added 2.6% after announcing that its net loss in the second quarter narrowed from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Panasonic\uc2 , which plummeted 19.5% on Thursday after releasing disappointing earnings, continued to fall, albeit at a slower pace -- down 0.7%.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi was 1.0% higher.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was just 0.1% higher, paring early gains as caution set in ahead of next week's risk events. Risk-aversion has characterized trading in Australia for much of the week, with local stocks underperforming regional peers -- down 0.2% week-to-date.\par \par \uc2 However, Wall Street's overnight gains bolstered Australian resource companies: \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  was 1.8% higher, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  added 2.0%, and \uc2 Fortescue Metals Group\uc2  rose 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 Write to Daniel Inman at Daniel.Inman@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 11-01-12 2145ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020121102e8b20000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
