\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Asian Shares Rise on US Cue; Hong Kong Hits 15-Month High\b0\par\par\uc2 1,464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2012\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA           13232.62   +136.16   +1.04% \par Nasdaq          3020.06   +42.83    +1.44% \par S&P 500         1427.59   +15.43    +1.09% \par FTSE 100        5861.92   +79.22    +1.37% \par Nikkei Stock    9058.27   +111.40   +1.25% \par Hang Seng      22067.66   +245.79   +1.13% \par Kospi           1916.90   +18.46    +0.97% \par SGX Nifty       5710.00   +40.50    +0.71% \par Nov. contract \par  \par USD/JPY    80.15-18   +0.48% \par Range      80.20-79.78 \par EUR/USD    1.2941-44  -0.14% \par Range      1.2982-1.2926 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Dec    $8.684 per bushel \par Comex Gold        $1,715.50    -$3.60 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,715.10-$1,727.50 \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude (NY)     $87.09    +$0.85 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks kicked off November with their biggest single-day rally in weeks, in a day packed with economic data that raised investors' spirits ahead of Friday's key jobs report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 136.16 points, or 1%, to 13232.62, on its second trading day after megastorm Sandy forced two days of market closures. The average marked its biggest one-day advance in both points and percentage terms since Sept. 13.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index rose 15.43 points, or 1.1%, to 1427.59, also posting its biggest gain since Sept. 13. The Nasdaq Composite gained 42.83 points, or 1.4%, to 3020.06.\par \par \uc2 Materials, technology and industrials shares were the top performers. The climb in the industrials sector was led by \uc2 Masco\uc2 , a provider of building supplies, which was one of the top gainers in the index. \uc2 Masco\uc2  has risen 10% since Sandy hit the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Tech giants Intel and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  propped up the \uc2 Dow\uc2 's gains, posting some of the largest increases in the average.\par \par \uc2 "Tech has been getting clobbered. So as people come back from being out of the office, tech is the place they are looking for deals," said Uri Landesman, president of New York hedge fund \uc2 Platinum Partners\uc2 , which oversees $1.1 billion in assets.\par \par \uc2 The rally was sparked by a line-up of economic reports, mostly positive, that pushed blue chips to a triple-digit increase in morning trade. A pair of labor-market reports boosted optimism ahead of Friday's employment data.\par \par \uc2 "These numbers give us the sense that it's going to be okay," said \uc2 J.J. Kinahan\uc2 , chief derivatives strategist at \uc2 TD Ameritrade\uc2 . "The economy is taking body punch after body punch, but it still seems pretty strong."\par \par \uc2 The Institute of Supply Management's reading on the manufacturing sector indicated that it expanded in October, and came in slightly higher than economists expected. The Conference Board reported that consumer confidence rose to its highest level since early 2008. Spending on construction projects rose in September as home building jumped, the Commerce Department reported.\par \par \uc2 Automatic Data Processing\uc2  reported 158,000 new private-sector jobs were created in October. The report didn't have a consensus expectation, since ADP switched to a new methodology. The report is seen as a preview to the closely watched government employment report due Friday.\par \par \uc2 "This certainly isn't enough to bring unemployment down, so it's discouraging in that sense. But it is encouraging that the job growth we're getting is broad-based across most parts of the economy," said Mark Zandi, chief economist for \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics, which helped develop the report's new methodology.\par \par \uc2 In earnings news, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  fell 1.3% after the pharmaceutical giant's third-quarter earnings and revenue came in well below analyst expectations.\par \par \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  was 0.5% higher after its third-quarter earnings and revenue exceeded analyst expectations.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian markets were higher on Friday, as positive U.S. economic data released Thursday contributed to growing optimism over the global economy, pushing Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index to its highest level since August 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regional stocks took their cue from Wall Street, where markets started November on an upbeat note following positive economic data. At the forefront was the Institute of Supply Management's reading on manufacturing, which showed a slightly better-than-expected expansion in October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. factory numbers followed Chinese manufacturing data, released early Thursday, which was taken as a sign that Asia's largest economy is recovering from its recent slowdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The next dose of economic data from the U.S. will be the government's employment report due later Friday. After that, the world's two largest economies will decide on their new leaders, with the U.S. Presidential election and the start of China's party congress both taking place next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong economic data helped the Hang Seng Index climb above the 20,000 mark for the first time in fifteen months. The index was up over 1%, benefiting from increased confidence in the Chinese economy as well as liquidity inflows into the city, as evidenced by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peg to the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are four stages in a stock market cycle, including hope, optimism, worry and disappointment. The Hong Kong market is in the hope stage," said Edward Huang, strategist at Haitong International in Hong Kong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Big movers in Hong Kong included Sands China, which was up 4.8%; retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Esprit Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which gained 3.2%; and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of East Asia\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which advanced 2.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In mainland China, the Shanghai Composite was 0.1% higher, moderating after jumping 1.7% on Thursday following the most recent Chinese manufacturing data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sharp fell 4.1% after posting a loss for the latest quarter and predicting a second straight year of record deficits; while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 3.3% after announcing that its net loss in the second quarter narrowed from a year earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Panasonic\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which plummeted 19.5% on Thursday after releasing disappointing earnings, continued to fall, albeit at a slower pace - down 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was just 0.2% higher, paring early gains as caution set in ahead of next week's risk events. Risk-aversion has characterized trading in Australia for much of the week, with local stocks underperforming regional peers - down less than 0.1% week-to-date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Wall Street's overnight gains bolstered Australian resource companies: \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 1.7% higher, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 2.0%, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fortescue Metals Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 1.5%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The increase in risk appetite was also evident in the movement in the yen, which weakened against the U.S. dollar. The U.S. dollar was at Y80.28 Friday, extending its 0.4% overnight rise against the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comex gold futures settled lower, giving up earlier gains as the dollar strengthened against the euro and brighter economic data damped investor appetite for the haven asset.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange closed higher after positive economic data sparked a buoyant session and dollar weakness in the London session helped to underpin gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  oil futures advanced Thursday after the U.S. government said oil stockpiles unexpectedly fell last week, while analysts warned tighter fuel supplies could be on the way in the wake of Hurricane Sandy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par Private Hiring, Fewer Layoffs Positive For Job Market \par U.S. Manufacturing Sector Barely Expands \par Buba's Dombret: Troika Must Decide Impartially if Greece Gets More Aid \par Fed's Rosengren: Fed Should Buy Bonds Until Unemployment Hits 7.25% \par Exxon's Profit Falls As Oil, Gas Production Drops \par AIG Posts $1.9 Bln 3Q Profit, Beats Expectations \par FSB Spells Out Capital Surcharges at Too-Big-To-Fail Banks \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Las Vegas Sands\f28 \uc2 \f2  Third-Quarter Net Down 18% on Higher Expenses \par Chesapeake Swings to 3rd-Quarter Loss on Write-Down \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Starbucks\f28 \uc2 \f2  Results Beat Expectations \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  May Cut More Than 10,000 Jobs \par OPEC Oil Output Falls In October \par Economy Has Been Stuck In Slow Growth, Lockhart Says \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pfizer\f28 \uc2 \f2  Profit Off 14%; Lipitor Sales Drop \par Ford Names Mark Fields Operating Chief \par Barrick CEO: Still in Talks to Sell African Barrick to China National Gold \par China's Internet Search Companies, Govt-Sponsored Trade Group Agree To Code Of Conduct \par Toyota US October Toyota Division Sales 135,392 Units, Up 16.8% \par American Honda: October U.S. Sales 106,973 Units, Up 8.8% \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2 : Oct U.S. Sales 79,685 Units, Down 3.2%> \par Codelco Accepts Mitsui Offer to Refinance $1.867B Anglo Sur Loan \par Honda to Invest $200M in Ohio Plants \par Docomo, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ally to Sell Business-Use Windows 8 Tablets -Kyodo \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-11-12 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 62500   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121102e8b20003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
