\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: China IRS Rises On Upbeat PMI; More Upside Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2012\par 09:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS are up after the country posted encouraging PMI data. The one-year IRS is 2 bps higher at 3.15%-3.25%, and the five-year IRS is up 3 bps at 3.36%-3.46%. China's official PMI rose to 50.2 in October from 49.8 in September, rebounding to expansionary territory after two months below 50. "PMI is just a start. I think it may be followed by other encouraging economic data for October, which will suggest a sharp rebound in the swaps," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. He expects the five-year IRS to rise to 3.5% in the near term. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0817 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts drift into negative territory at the open, with the move likely linked to comments from BOE Deputy Governor Charlie Bean who highlighted late Wednesday that the effect of more QE on demand at this juncture is questionable, noted \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , who expected Bean not to support further QE at the November MPC meeting. Ahead, manufacturing PMI for October is released at 0930 GMT, with the headline index expected to edge down to 48.0 from 48.4 in September. Later the DMO will auction GBP900M of ultra-long dated index-linked gilts at 1030 GMT, with recent concession building and cheap cross-market valuations supporting the sale. The December contract is 0.15 lower at 118.99, the 10-year cash yields 1.875%, while the 10-year UK/Germany spread widens out to 40.5 bps. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] China's government bonds close flat, shrugging off the PBOC's biggest ever weekly fund injection, as investors are still concerned that the large amount of reverse repos due to mature next week will prompt tighter liquidity conditions, says a Shenzhen-based trader with a local insurer. Banks need to repay CNY533 billion worth of reverse repos to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week, a record-high weekly repayment. The \uc2 Shanghai Stock Exchange\uc2  government bond index ends at 135.21, unchanged from Wednesday's close, and is tipped to hover around 135.2 in the near term. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  fear premium trading higher for one-week expiries at Thursday's European open. The period now encompasses not only Friday's US payrolls data but also next week's ECB, RBA and BOE policy meetings- any of which have potential to generate greater price movement than seen in recent trading sessions. In the leading EUR/USD pair, one-week protection costs are trading at 8.75%- 0.5 of a volatility point firmer from Wednesday. Similar size gains are also displayed in the EUR/JPY, EUR/GBP, AUD/USD and USD/JPY pairings. However, while investors are bracing for some choppy near-term price action, there is little interest to buy protection in the fixed-dated tenors where implied volatilities remain under downward pressure.(david.willmer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0808 GMT [Dow Jones] The UK's index-linked gilt auction should pass smoothly, given the evidence of a recent concession for this supply, RBC says. The DMO is scheduled to sell GBP900 million notional of the i/l 0.75% Nov 2047 at 1030 GMT, with RBC noting that longer-dated linkers are looking cheap when regressed against the yields on conventional gilts, while this issue is also cheap on a cross-market basis against the US TIPS 0.75% Feb 2042. The only drawback is that the DMO has already confirmed that the next syndication, planned for the end of November, will be a tap of the neighboring UKTi 0.125% Mar 2044, and "there is limited scope for a strong performance coming out of the auction in this sector, as the market focuses on an expected GBP3.75bn nominal supply of IL44s."(nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0802 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi falls 0.7% to 1898.44, ending two sessions of gains, as local institutions sell heavyweights such as \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE), down 3.8% at KRW216,000, and Posco (005490.SE), 2.2% lower at KRW336,000. Local institutions are net sellers of KRW355.9 billion worth of shares on Thursday alone. Elsewhere, \uc2 KB Financial Group\uc2  (105560.SE) drops 2.4% to KRW36,200 with expectations slowly turning to disappointment as the deal to buy \uc2 ING Groep N.V\uc2 . (ING)'s Korean life insurance business is delayed. "With major U.S. and local corporate earnings all out now and big events (power shifts in the U.S. and China) left for next week, trading would have to turn tepid to make a limited technical rebound," says \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  in a report. Analysts tip the index to hover around 1900 in the near term. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNH, or offshore USD/CNY is slightly lower as the latest China PMI data shows signs of a recovery. The offshore pair is last quoted at 6.2420 vs 6.2435 late Wednesday in Asia. "The latest China PMI data boosts investors' confidence in China's economy, keeping the USD/CNH under pressure," says a senior trader at a local bank. He tips the offshore pair to trade between 6.2380-6.2440 in the near term. The Hong Kong Treasury Markets Association set the USD/CNH fixing at 6.2433 vs Wednesday's 6.2455.(carol.chan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds open the session barely moved ahead of the final reading of euro-zone manufacturing PMI at 0900 GMT, which is expected to come in around consensus of 45.3. In US hours, ADP employment, initial claims and manufacturing PMI will all hold the market's interest. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  notes Dec Bunds have "repeatedly run into technical resistance in the 141.85-142.00 area, which suggests that a slide back to the 140 area could be on the cards near term," although a break through resistance could lead to "a bounce into the 143+ area." There is no euro-zone supply scheduled Thursday, while the EU's short-selling ban of uncovered government bonds and CDS will become effective today. The December contract is 0.03 higher at 141.71 and the 10-year cash is quoted at 1.46%. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] December Bunds face resistance at 141.91 says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  and again at 142.16. Support for the contract is at 140.89 and 140.67. December Bunds are up 0.07 at 141.75. (tommy.stubbington@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0706 GMT [Dow Jones] The HKMA's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's rise against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --a clear sign of continued liquidity inflows--is helping to further boost the city's benchmark, which is now up 0.6% at 21,761. If the last round of liquidity inflows after the first round of U.S. quantitative easing was any measure, there remains significantly more room for more hot money to flow into Hong Kong, notes Francis Lun of Lyncean Holdings. "There's also a lot of speculative inflows betting on further gains in the Chinese yuan," he says, as the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has for years been seen as a proxy for the yuan's movements, which isn't freely convertible. Lun says the continued inflows will help further lift the benchmark index, pointing at next resistance level at 23,000. (jeffrey.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are 0.1% lower midday at 1298.04, with telecom stocks leading the market's decline following news that the NBTC (National Broadcasting and Telecommunications Commission) will delay granting 3G licences to the three bid winners amid allegations of collusion at a recent auction, which three mobile phone operators deny the allegations. "I think there is still a risk for a market correction and it could head lower to test the 1260 level," says \uc2 Thanachart Securities\uc2  analyst Adisak Phupiphathirungul. Support is tipped at 1292, while resistance is pegged at 1300. Among top actives, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) is up 2.7% at THB384, \uc2 Advanced Info Service (ADVANC.TH) \uc2 is down 2.3% at THB193, Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) is down 0.3% at THB176.50, Bangkok Land (BLAND.TH) is up 1.5% at THB1.34 and \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) is up 0.3% at THB179.50. (payungsak.wiriyabunditkul@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mixed; the Nikkei adds 0.1%, the S&P/ASX drops 1.3%, the HSI rises 0.5%, the Kospi slips 0.8%, the Sensex is off 0.2%, the Shanghai Composite jumps 1.8%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen is weaker versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after data showed manufacturing activity in China improved and as speculators resume yen selling amid expectations for further easing by the BOJ. The USD/JPY is at 80.04 from 79.77 late Wednesday in New York, the EUR/USD is at 1.2958 from 1.2960, and the EUR/JPY is at 103.72 from 103.37. China's official manufacturing PMI rises to 50.2 in October from September's 49.8, just shy of the 50.3 estimate. The final reading on \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's China manufacturing PMI is revised up to an eight-month high of 49.5 for October from a preliminary reading of 49.1, higher than September's final reading of 47.9. South Korea's CPI rises 2.1% on-year in October, faster than September's 2.0% rise but below expectations for a 2.2% increase. South Korea posts an October trade surplus of $3.8 billion in October, above expectations for a $3.45 billion surplus. Australia's 3Q export price index fall 6.4% on-quarter, due mainly to declines in the price of industrial commodities, signaling the resource-rich economy may be slowing faster than previously thought. The average price of housing in 100 major Chinese cities rose in October from September, marking the fifth consecutive monthly increase, though the gains were slight due to lackluster sales. Spot gold is at $1,719.90/oz, down 30 cents from the NY close. December Nymex crude oil futures are up 10 cents at $86.34/bbl. (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 01, 2012 04:19 ET (08:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121101e8b10020c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
