\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Has Hong Kong popped its bubble?\b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2012\par 09:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 If this is a fair characterization of Hong Kong property, it is the drivers of investment demand that matter most. Have the fundamentals really changed?\par \par \uc2 Investors will likely be reconsidering political risk and the potential for further government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Financial Secretary John Tsang said on Friday the government was ready to introduce further measures, which would likely include further taxes.\par \par \uc2 But for mainland Chinese investors, there are an assortment of reasons supporting property investment in Hong Kong from offshoring money to a more attractive tax regime.\par \par \uc2 The other gorilla in the room is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  play. The peg of the Hong Kong dollar to the U.S. dollar has been an important dynamic in mainland Chinese investing.\par \par \uc2 For one, it means Hong Kong property has appeared less expensive after the yuan has gained over 30% against the dollar in recent years.\par \par \uc2 And keeping some money or assets in Hong Kong also gives all investors an option on a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 A longstanding belief is the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should eventually be pegged to the yuan or a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The timing, however, is not expected before China is ready to make the yuan fully convertible. But this may not be just up to Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Another risk with the latest move is that it again flags to international investors the stresses facing the 29-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg as its strains under "hot money" flows. Arguably, it is at the root of local asset inflation by forcing the importation of low interest rates and a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  value from the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 10 days, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening by selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars.\par \par \uc2 Some investors will interpret the new tax as sign such pressures are escalating.\par \par \uc2 There have already been questions about how effective the tax will be in curbing wider capital flows. Some property experts predict the tax will simply divert investment into commercial and industrial property. There have already been some signs of this, as investors try to skirt stamp-duty resale restrictions in the residential market.\par \par \uc2 Higher prices here will also be a problem. It would mean increased costs for businesses at a time when exports, retail sales and stock-market turnover have all been declining.\par \par \uc2 All said, Hong Kong will struggle to find a painless way out of its asset bubble.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020121029e8at0030e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
