\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(10/26) Once Staid, Yuan Starts To Swing -3-\b0\par\par\uc2 1,247 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2012\par 04:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA)\par \par \uc2 By Lingling Wei\par \par \uc2 BEIJING -- The once-predictable Chinese yuan has become increasingly volatile, a development that signals a stronger role for markets in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves but poses greater \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks for businesses and investors alike.\par \par \uc2 China still keeps a tight grip on the yuan, also known as the renminbi, by limiting its trading within a range. In terms of volatility, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still pales in comparison to more freely traded counterparts.\par \par \uc2 But since late September, as China's economy started to show signs of stabilizing from a slowdown earlier in the year, traders betting on the yuan's rise have pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the upper limit of the Beijing-imposed trading range more frequently than in the past, according to an analysis by The Wall Street Journal. The intraday moves have been much bigger than suggested by changes in the daily rate set by \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders say they are increasingly taking advantage of an April move by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  to expand the yuan's trading territory.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the yuan again hit the upper limit of its daily trading band, finishing at the day's high of 6.2417 to the dollar. The level is the highest since the launch of modern \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in China in 1994.\par \par \uc2 China's lighter hand on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is one reason why the yuan has experienced a rare bumpy ride this year. The yuan is now up 0.8% against the U.S. dollar for the year, after falling as much as 1.6% in July. Back then, as investors worried over China's economic slowdown, traders sold the yuan, aiding Beijing's effort to make it weaker against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In effect, the new volatility underscores the increasing willingness of Beijing to allow market forces to play a bigger role in setting the yuan's value, though the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  still guides the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's longer-term moves. The volatility shows that "the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  much these days, and the market is taking full advantage of the widened trading band," said analyst WoonKhien Chia at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PBOC\uc2  officials didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's recent rise comes as China prepares for a once-a-decade leadership change set to begin next month. Some market analysts expect the transition to pave the way for more monetary-easing measures aimed at keeping China's economy humming. Stimulus measures would typically put downward pressure on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but any increase in optimism over the economy could help strengthen the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has been buffeted by the same forces hitting other Asian markets. Money has poured into Asian assets as investors seek higher returns in response to declines in interest rates that have resulted from Western central banks' efforts to aid growth.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times in the past week to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar after it hit the top of its trading band against the U.S. dollar. Shares in Hong Kong hit a 14-month high Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Chinese stocks have risen recently as well after a dismal year, in part because of the inflow of new cash. Net flows of cash into Hong Kong and mainland China equity mutual funds have totaled $1.3 billion over the past four weeks, the most for any part of Asia excluding Japan, according to research by \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have said the country will push ahead with establishing a market-based exchange-rate system, whereby the yuan would move up and down like any other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The change is part of China's broader plan to overhaul its creaky financial sector, elevate the country's status in the international monetary system and someday challenge the U.S. dollar as the de facto global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Russian Prime Minister \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said Thursday that Russia and China should move toward settling trade in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that full convertibility for the yuan is "a matter of time."\par \par \uc2 China's growing flexibility also could help deflect criticism by the U.S., where the yuan's value has become an issue in the current election campaign. Some U.S. businesses and lawmakers say China undervalues the yuan to make its exports more attractive abroad. Republican contender Mitt Romney has pledged that if he is elected, he will label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on his first day in office.\par \par \uc2 The increased swings are causing problems for exporters like Du Hanbing, who runs a business in the southern Chinese city of Shenzhen that makes embossing machines and sells them in the U.S. and Canada. Mr. Du said he usually would adjust prices based on how the yuan is trading.\par \par \uc2 "If the yuan depreciates, I would bump up the dollar prices for our products, and vice versa," he said. "But nowadays, the yuan seems to be switching courses faster than we could adjust our prices."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Du said the company hasn't tried to hedge against the yuan's swings because that could be expensive for a small business like his.\par \par \uc2 The volatility has increased the yuan's appeal for financial investors like hedge funds that profit from swings either way. Andy Seaman, a portfolio manager at hedge-fund firm Stratton Street Capital, said he has been increasing his exposure to renminbi all year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Seaman manages a yuan-bond fund with about $180 million in assets under management. But the London-based fund doesn't hold any yuan bonds due to what he views as a lack of liquidity in Hong Kong's yuan-bond market. Rather, it buys Asian dollar-denominated bonds and enters into forward contracts that allow the fund to get renminbi by selling dollars.\par \par \uc2 The fund is up 2.8% this month and nearly 23% this year, he said. It has benefited from both yuan appreciation and rising yields on the underlying bonds.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Seaman said the fund has also benefited from the yuan's increased volatility. Prices on the yuan-linked forward contracts he buys are lower than they otherwise would be, reflecting the uncertain outlook, leaving his fund with bigger gains if the yuan continues to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 "Money is flowing from the debtor nations to the creditor nations, such as China, building up the pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as renminbi, to appreciate," he said.\par \par \uc2 The new volatility doesn't preclude Beijing from taking a stronger hand. A year ago, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  pushed the yuan to strengthen against the dollar even as investors tested its trading band by pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the other way.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  keeps a leash on the yuan's trading in mainland China by setting a daily rate for it, known as the parity rate. In April, it widened the range in which the yuan is allowed to rise and fall daily to 1% above or below the parity rate from 0.5% previously. The move reflected China's belief that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fairly valued and ready to take a more prominent role globally.\par \par \uc2 "The wider band was seen as a step for the spot rate to be more flexible, allowing greater volatility," said Paul Mackel, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Wynne Wang and Stephen Fidler contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 26-10-12 0257GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020121026e8aq0006z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
