\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  GLOBAL CALL: Our Take on the Day's Big  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  News\b0\par\par\uc2 709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2012\par 13:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Global Call covers the main talking points from a news meeting involving DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader editors in New York, London and Singapore, as well as other \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hot spots when warranted.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michael Casey\f28 \par \par \uc2 1. Japanese authorities are finally getting their way. Dollar/yen got above Y80 for the first time in three-and-a-half months during Tokyo trading as speculation continued to mount over the prospect of action to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to combat a softening economy. Yet one factor in that gain was a somewhat flimsy report in a Japanese newspaper, which claimed that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  was planning on increasing its asset purchases by a whopping Y20 trillion at its next meeting. The report was later widely denied by Japanese officials and the dollar later dipped below Y80 again. But given their persistent efforts to talk down the yen, there must be some relief inside the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  that dollar has at least briefly been quoted with an 80 handle again.\par \par \uc2 2. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  again injected funds into the money market in what has been its chosen method of adding monetary stimulus for some weeks. But analysts are expecting the market to show a net drain on liquidity by the end of the week as banks' prior borrowings from the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will mature. With QE3 having sent foreign inflows back into the region and some indicators suggesting that China's slowdown may have bottomed, there mightn't be much more stimulus coming from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some hopeful souls are still betting that it will cut banks' reserve requirement ratios, but there could be too much flux in the political process right now for that to happen, with the leadership transition still uncompleted.\par \par \uc2 3. In a mark of how QE3 is having this effect on the region, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second time in recent days to defend the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the dollar. The inflows have pushed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the top end of its trading band, which compels the HKMA to step in. Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangement, these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  amount to naked injections of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity into the local banking system and have the effect of ensuring that local interest rates stay very close to zero. In recent years that has spurred periods of intense property price appreciation as well as consumer inflation and so has occasionally stirred debate over the prospect of Hong Kong abandoning the peg. But that would do such profound damage to Hong Kong's peg-dependent financial system that very few people foresee this coming anytime soon. In the meantime, expect routine HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 4. This time it was Reserve Bank of Australia board member \uc2 Heather Ridout\uc2 's turn to warn that the Australian economy is slowing and that the Australian dollar must fall. Coming on the back of comments to us Monday from her colleague \uc2 Jillian Broadbent\uc2 , Ms. Ridout's comments have reinforced the view that the RBA is gearing up for another rate cut.\par \par \uc2 5. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgraded the debt of five Spanish regions, which put the euro under a modicum of pressure. But with Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy widely expected to ask for an EU bailout, which would trigger bond-buying by the ECB, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are mostly stuck in a holding pattern.\par \par \uc2 6. It's also quiet in the Americas today. The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  will start and finish its policy meeting Tuesday and is not expected to make any change to its 1% benchmark rate. The Fed starts the first of a two-day meeting in which most economists similarly do not expect it to do anything [largely because it only just recently embarked on its game-shifting third round of quantitative easing.] The Treasury sells $35 billion in two-year notes and the Richmond Fed releases its manufacturing survey results.\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 Michael Casey\uc2  is managing editor for the Americas at DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  news service jointly produced by Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.)\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 23-10-12 1141GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121023e8an000od\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
