\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Mostly Lower; Utility Plays Struggle In Tokyo\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chris Oliver   \par \uc2 915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2012\par 12:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Asia stocks traded in a downbeat fashion Tuesday as dividend worries weighed on Japanese utility firms while concerns over lackluster third-quarter earnings dragged on shares in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei put in a flat performance, while the Shanghai Composite Index lost 0.9% and South Korea's Kospi declined 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index managed to gain 0.1%, and Singapore's Straits Times Index was up 0.2%. Markets were closed for a holiday in Hong Kong, which made new headlines on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  front.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for a second time in less than a week on Tuesday, stepping in to sell the Hong Kong dollar in an attempt to halt appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it traded near the lower channel of its trading band against the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 The authority sold $3.914 billion Hong Kong dollars ($505 million), on the heels of having sold last Friday HK$4.67 billion during New York trading, its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2009.\par \par \uc2 Most Asian markets have gained so far this month, gains that have been made against emerging Asian economies that "are not really booming but [are] still quite robust," said Lucinda Chan. division director at Macquarie Private Wealth.\par \par \uc2 In addition, U.S. earnings results out in recent days "have been quite reasonable," she said, while the U.S. economy has shown some signs of improvement.\par \par \uc2 The third and final debate between President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and Republican challenger \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  took place during Asian morning trade. U.S. stock futures edged lower after the debate wrapped up, then deepened further.\par \par \uc2 Both contenders commented on China, with Obama saying he will insist that Beijing play by the same rules as everyone else. Romney echoed those comments and reiterated his pledge to immediately label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator should he be elected.\par \par \uc2 "Coming into tonight's debate, President Obama retained a small structural advantage, and I suspect that he will retain that advantage after this debate," said Nomura economist Lewis Alexander in a note.\par \par \uc2 Dividend concerns weighed on the Japanese utility sector, as \uc2 Kansai Electric Power Co\uc2 . fell 13% after a Nikkei report that the electricity supplier won't pay a dividend at the end of the fiscal year. This would be its first dividend omission in 61 years. \uc2 Kansai Electric\uc2 's rivals also traded weak, with shares of \uc2 Chubu Electric Power Co\uc2 . shedding 9.9% and \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 . falling 1.5%.\par \par \uc2 Energy-sector firms likewise declined in Tokyo, with \uc2 JX Holdings Inc\uc2 . down 1.6% and \uc2 Japan Petroleum Exploration Co\uc2 . down 1%. The pullback came after a 1.5% drop for crude-oil futures in New York on Monday.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the U.S. dollar briefly traded over 80 Japanese yen for the first time since early July, up from Y79.91 on Monday, before falling back to Y79.77 as immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -easing hopes faded.\par \par \uc2 The euro reached its highest point against the yen since May but then also retreated. Despite the yen's rebound, the dollar was still at relatively elevated levels against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and some exporters held their gains in Tokyo on Tuesday, with \uc2 Casio Computer Co\uc2 . trading up 2.8%, \uc2 Nikon Corp\uc2 . adding 1.6% and \uc2 Panasonic Corp\uc2 . nudging ahead by 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, shares of \uc2 Fuji Heavy Industries\uc2 , maker of Subaru brand vehicles, gained 3.8% after raising its net profit view to Y40 billion from a prior estimate of Y23 billion.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, steel makers were adding pressure to the market, with Posco, the world's fourth-largest producer, trading 2.1% lower ahead of its earnings report due late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgraded its long-term-debt rating on Posco to BBB+ from A- late Monday, saying that the firm will struggle to secure financing and that its \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  performance will likely be weaker than previously expected. Among its rivals, \uc2 Hyundai Steel Co\uc2 . declined 1.7% while \uc2 Dongbu Steel Ltd\uc2 . eased 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Mainland-listed Chinese commodity firms were weak, with \uc2 Aluminum Corp. of China\uc2  trading down 1% in Shanghai. Mainland steel makers were also lower amid expected sluggish earnings and what's believed to be government plans for a forced consolidation of the sector. Shares of \uc2 Angang Steel Co\uc2 . fell 1.4% as \uc2 Baoshan Iron & Steel\uc2  retreated 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Air China Ltd\uc2 . dropped 1% after abandoning a plan to raise funds in a private placement with \uc2 China National Aviation Holding Co\uc2 ., its state-owned parent. Tuesday's session marked the first for the Beijing-based carrier since its shares were suspended earlier in October.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, commodity extractors underpinned gains for Sydney stocks, with \uc2 Oz Minerals Ltd\uc2 . up 1.9% after saying that it's on track to meet its fiscal-year production targets for copper and gold. Also in the sector, \uc2 Fortescue Metals Group Ltd\uc2 . advanced 1.7% while \uc2 Rio Tinto Ltd\uc2 . rose 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 23-10-12 1032GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121023e8an000ku\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
