\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Hong Kong's Not off the Peg\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aaron Back  \par \uc2 460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2012\par 12:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's U.S. dollar peg is under threat again. It will surely withstand the challenge for a long while yet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The peg, in place since 1983, was pushed to its limit last week. Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising above its permitted band, the first such action in nearly three years.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that loose monetary policies around the world, as well as improving market sentiment toward China, have sent capital flowing into Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 By tying its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar, Hong Kong gives up the ability to conduct its own monetary policy. The peg means Hong Kong mirrors the ultra-low interest rates of the U.S. and risks high inflation and asset bubbles, particularly in the volatile property market. Earlier this year, no less an authority than Joseph Yam, the former manager of the HKMA, said that Hong Kong should consider alternatives such as Singapore's system of setting the exchange rate against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But any change to the peg risks substantial disruption, especially at a time when global markets are still uncertain. The peg underpins the entire Hong Kong financial system. The case for a switch would have to be overwhelming--and it is not. Comparing inflation rates in Hong Kong and Singapore, the evidence for one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system over the other is not clear-cut. For instance, over the past 15 years, Hong Kong's inflation rate has been more volatile than Singapore's but it has also been much lower too, on average. The property markets in Singapore and Hong Kong are prone to big fluctuations, and authorities in both cities are fretting over soaring prices.\par \par \uc2 The peg must surely go eventually. Linking Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the yuan is a real possibility--though only once the Chinese unit becomes a fully viable international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that could be some way off. For now, there is too much downside for the monetary authorities to give the go-ahead to such a major change. Hong Kong's dollar peg is under pressure. But it is not likely to buckle in the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.)\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\uc2  [ 10-22-12 0646ET ]\par \par \uc2 5090  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121022e8am0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
