\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: U.S. Stocks Edge Higher; Late-Session Rally Erases Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 2,399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2012\par 00:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong markets are closed for Cheung Yeung Festival, while Thai markets are closed for Chulalongkorn Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      13345.89  +2.38  +0.02% \par Nasdaq     3016.96 +11.34  +0.38% \par S&P 500    1433.81  +0.62  +0.04% \par FTSE 100   5882.91 -13.24  -0.22% \par Xetra DAX  7328.05 -52.59  -0.71% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 28/32  -1/32 0.311% +1.2BP \par 5Y Tsy   99 7/32  -5/32 0.787% +3.6BP \par 10Y Tsy 98 11/32 -12/32 1.810% +4.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY    79.94-98  +0.86% \par Range      79.95-79.22 \par EUR/USD    1.3059-62  +0.30% \par Range      1.3083-1.3016 \par AUD/USD    1.0324-28  +0.08% \par Range      1.0348-1.0298 \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,726.30    +$2.30 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,747.40-$1,730.30 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $88.73    -$1.32 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rallied in the last hour of trading to eke out slim gains, as investors weighed corporate earnings reports and shrugged off a reduced profit forecast from industrial bellwether Caterpillar.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 2.38 points, or less than 0.1%, to 13345.89. The blue-chip benchmark rebounded from losses of as much as 108 points, following a 205-point drop on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index tacked on 0.62 point, or less than 0.1%, to 1433.81. Technology shares led gains across four of the index's 10 sectors. The Nasdaq Composite Index rose 11.34 points, or 0.4%, to 3016.96.\par \par \uc2 "One thing this market has shown me is a remarkable resilience," said Uri Landesman, president of New York hedge fund \uc2 Platinum Partners\uc2 , which oversees more than $1 billion in assets. "The bulls have weathered every single haymaker. I would have expected it to go down, but it really hangs in there."\par \par \uc2 Peabody Energy\uc2  jumped 12%, leading the S&P 500, after the coal miner gave a current-year forecast that topped analysts' projections. The shares bucked selling among energy stocks as front-month natural gas and crude-oil futures fell.\par \par \uc2 VF Corp\uc2 . slid 4.4% as the maker of North Face jackets and Wrangler jeans reported slowing revenue growth in Europe.\par \par \uc2 SunTrust Banks\uc2  fell 3.4% after reporting third-quarter revenue dropped, excluding the effect of a securities gain.\par \par \uc2 Caterpillar rose 1.5% after the world's largest heavy-machinery maker by sales reported a third-quarter profit that topped analysts' forecasts. Caterpillar reduced its 2012 earnings and revenue outlook given a weaker-than-anticipated global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Earnings are topping out, there's no question," said John Fox, director of research at Fenimore Asset Management, which oversees $1.5 billion in assets. "It's been a tug of war for the last few weeks. That's one side of the rope, and the other side is \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity."\par \par \uc2 In other corporate news, \uc2 Monster Beverage\uc2  tumbled 14%, the biggest drop among S&P 500 components after the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported the Food and Drug Administration has received reports of five deaths "possibly linked to" an energy drink made by the company. A Monster spokeswoman said the company wasn't aware of any FDA reports about deaths after people consumed its popular energy drinks, aside from one pertaining to a girl whose mother is suing the company.\par \par \uc2 Ancestry.com\uc2  rallied 8% after the genealogy website agreed to be acquired by European private-equity firm \uc2 Permira\uc2  in a deal valued at about $1.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Yahoo Inc\uc2 . rose nearly 3% to $16.24 in after hours trading after the company reported a sharp rise in third-quarter earnings as the Internet media firm recorded a big gain from its recent sale of shares in Chinese Internet giant \uc2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\uc2 . Search and display revenue improved slightly.\par \par \uc2 Asian shares were mixed Monday as investors consolidated their positions following the recent rally, but the Hong Kong market posted healthy gains amid signs of increased capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market Friday for the first time in three years to weaken the Hong Kong dollar so as to maintain its peg with the U.S. dollar. This happened after weeks of capital inflows pushed up the value of the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Judging from past experiences, whenever this happens, the local stock market tends to have a solid performance afterwards," said \uc2 Alvin Cheung\uc2 , associate director at Prudential Brokerage. Mr. Cheung expects the Hang Seng Index's uptrend to continue until the month-end, with 21,760 the short-term target.\par \par \uc2 That view was shared by ANZ Research, which said in a note that it believed "the strong inflow into the (Hong Kong dollar) money market relates to the recovery of global investors' sentiment towards \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market." It added that the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  "may foretell a rally of other asset classes, similar to the situation after the global financial crisis stabilized."\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended slightly higher Monday. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index is likely to trade between 2100 and 2250 this week because of expectations the government will seek a steady rise in the benchmark ahead of the 18th Communist Party Congress in early November.\par \par \uc2 Investors will also be eyeing the release of third-quarter earnings, with major companies beginning to release their results this week.\par \par \uc2 "The index is currently undergoing a short-term consolidation, but the general trend is upwards, given the green shoots we see in the Chinese economy, as well as the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in share buybacks in the larger firms," said Zhang Yanbin, an analyst at Zheshang Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks eked out a positive finish Monday, as poor Japan trade data and heightened expectations for policy-easing steps by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  pushed the yen down, helping exporters.\par \par \uc2 "Confidence in technology sector profits has eroded somewhat, making it difficult for the Nikkei to post significant gains," said Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "Foreign investors are also loathe to do much more than short-covering in Japan shares if overseas bourses, including China's, are not positive."\par \par \uc2 U.S. oil futures fell to their lowest level in two weeks Monday, dragged down by weaker equities and persistent concerns about economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Comex gold futures inched higher as some foreign investors benefited from a weaker dollar while others prepared for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy-setting meeting.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange closed lower amid quiet trading conditions and a gloomy start to the week for investor risk sentiment.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar jumped Monday to its highest level against the yen in over three months, after weak Japanese trade data raised expectations for further \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar traded up to Y79.97, its strongest level against the yen since July 12. It ended the day at Y79.95, up from Y79.32 late Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan posted a wider-than-expected $7.2 billion deficit in September, its third consecutive trade deficit. Japan's territorial dispute with China over islands in the East China Sea took its toll on the domestic economy as exports to China dropped 14.1% in September from a year earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  also downgraded its economic outlook for eight of Japan's nine regions in October, noting in its quarterly report that the economic recovery has "paused or moderated" in these regions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With the situation getting worse and worse in Japan and the BoJ already reducing forecasts, it'd be surprising for the BoJ not to ease," said Brian Kim, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at RBS in Stamford, Conn. "People are expecting some form of easing, but the question is how much."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has already appreciated 2.6% against the yen this month on expectations the BoJ will ramp up the size of its asset-purchase program, which was increased by Y10 trillion at the bank's last meeting in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro also rose to a 5 1/2-month high against the yen of Y104.46, before ending the session at Y104.40 from Y103.26.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the euro edged higher against the dollar after German Prime Minister Angela Merkel told the Wall Street Journal that Germany is likely to support extending the deadline for Greece to meet its fiscal austerity targets. Greece remains locked in negotiations with international creditors over whether it should receive the next tranche of its bailout loan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro last traded at $1.3061, up from $1.3023 late Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar dipped against the greenback to $1.0322 from $1.0332.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  begins its scheduled two-day policy meeting, its first since announcing a new round of quantitative easing in September. While Fed statements are generally market-moving events, "this week's meeting should be less dramatic and less of an issue for markets since most of the fireworks from the Fed came last month," said Robert Lynch, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, which measures the dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , edged up to 69.772 from 69.756.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys came under pressure Monday as investors aimed to push down prices heading into this week's $99 billion supply of U.S. government bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Without fresh economic data to guide trading, Treasury investors focused on preparing for the upcoming auctions, which kick off with a $35 billion two-year notes sale Tuesday. That is followed by a $35 billion five-year offering Wednesday and $29 billion seven-year sale Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond analysts broadly expect these auctions to be greeted with decent interest given the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's efforts to keep borrowing rates suppressed in its bid to stimulate the economy. That ultra-accommodative policy should keep Treasurys in constant demand, even amid recent signs that the U.S. economy is improving and as tensions surrounding Europe's debt crisis abate, marking an easing of two factors that have fueled relentless appetite for safe-haven Treasurys over the past year. [ 22-10-12 2201GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The 'flight to quality' trade has dissipated with some calmness in Europe, recent stronger data and the contemplation on where rates should be if there is a change in the White House," said Justin Lederer, senior trader of interest rates at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cantor Fitzgerald\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "However with this said, there still should be a safe-haven bid, the Fed is still easing and growth remains subdued."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late-afternoon trading, two-year notes fell 1/32 in price to yield 0.311% and 10-year notes lost 12/32 to yield 1.810%. Five-year notes shed 5/32 to yield 0.787%, while seven-year notes dropped 10/32 to yield 1.243%. Bond yields rise when prices fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds closed higher Monday as liquidity in the money market remained abundant, a Beijing-based trader with a local brokerage said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, bond prices are unlikely to rise much further in the near term amid expectations that the country's economic growth had bottomed in the third quarter, he said. The PBOC-backed Financial News also said in a front-page article Monday that there are currently no grounds for further loosening of monetary policy in China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is expected to hover around current levels in coming days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds edged up slightly Monday as weak U.S. share prices outweighed strong Japan equities as a trading factor for JGB investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, investors will likely remain on the sidelines ahead of the Bank of Japan's policy-setting meeting next week. Most investors have factored in some kind of additional easing action by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The focus is now on what exactly it will do.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Just buying more long-term JGBs, which is our main scenario, won't be enough to support the weakening Japanese economy and that will give birth to further speculation that the BOJ will be forced to do more in the future," \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBS Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief rates strategist Akito Fukunaga said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday's Asian calendar, Singapore releases consumer price figures for September, Taiwan has industrial output data for the same month, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sri Lanka's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announces its monetary policy review.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Caterpillar Net Up 49% On Gain \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  3rd-Quarter Profit Soars on Alibaba Gain \par Rosneft To Buy Entirety Of \f28 \uc2 \f2 TNK-BP\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Petronas Will Still Pursue Progress Energy Deal \par Greek Premier Ousts Lawmaker \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pfizer\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Buy Maker Of Liquid ADHD Drug \par Greece Posts Current Account Surplus In August \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fedex\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sees 10% Shipment Growth For 'Green Monday' \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siemens\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Exit Solar Energy In Overhaul \par EU Refuses French Request to Monitor Imports of Korea Cars \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air China\f28 \uc2 \f2  Terminates Plan to Raise Fund from Parent \par Esprit: To Raise up to HK$5.25 Bln via Rights Issue \par Toyota Industries to Buy 100% of Shrs of Cascade at $65 Per Shr In Cash \par EU to Ease Restrictions on Japan Food Imports From Nov. 1-Kyodo \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0001  US           World Bank and IFC's 'Doing Business' annual report \par 0200  Sri Lanka    Oct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Sri Lanka\f28 \uc2 \f2  Monetary Policy Review Announcement \par 0500  Singapore    Sep CPI \par 0645  France       Oct Monthly business survey (goods-producing industries) \par 0800  Taiwan       Sep Industrial output \par 0830  UK           Sep BBA main high street banking groups statistics \par 1145  US           ICSC-\f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chain Store Sales Index \par 1255  US           Johnson Redbook Retail Sales Index \par 1400  EU           Oct FCCI Flash Consumer Confidence Indicator \par 1400  US           Sep Mass Layoffs \par 1400  US           Oct Richmond Fed Business Activity S