\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(10/22) Hong Kong Takes Action To Weaken Its Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2012\par 01:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C9\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Fiona Law \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG -- After weeks of capital inflows from abroad that have pushed up the value of the Hong Kong dollar to the top of its trading band, the city's de facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday to weaken the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the first time in nearly three years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve's latest round of stimulus measures and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent pledge to buy sovereign bonds has restored global investor confidence and led money managers to buy a wide range of assets around the world, including in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The influx of money has pushed up the Hang Seng Index -- the benchmark of local shares that is closely tied to the fortunes of China -- to a six-month high, making it one of Asia's best performers in 2012.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 4.67 billion Hong Kong dollars (US$602.6 million) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday during New York trading as the U.S. dollar hit HK$7.7500, the upper limit of where the Hong Kong dollar is allowed to trade against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The move on Friday was small and not surprising to traders, given that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trading around HK$7.76 for weeks. The HKMA undertakes to buy or sell the local unit whenever it touches either side of the band that ranges from HK$7.75 to HK$7.85.\par \par \uc2 Still, the reason for this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- improved global risk appetite -- was different from the last time Hong Kong stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, during the height of the financial crisis. From October 2008 to December 2009, the HKMA repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, as the dollar-pegged local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as a haven asset amid global market turmoil.\par \par \uc2 An HKMA spokeswoman said Saturday that there had been robust demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, driven by a stabilized European market and a weak U.S. dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes as other central banks in the region also have sought to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening further to help keep their exports competitively priced. The Bank of Japan has recently threatened to slow the yen's appreciation, while \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  is suspected of curbing the won's rally. The Hong Kong dollar is also partly seen as a proxy for China's yuan, also called the renminbi, which is trading near a 19-year high against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Capital inflow to Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue, at least through the end of this year, should the greenback remain weak ahead of the U.S. presidential election in November. The Hong Kong dollar will be one of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain bolstered, as it's more liquid, and the renminbi is strengthening," said Woody Chan, treasurer of Citic Bank Corp.'s \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit. "Further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the HKMA could be seen in the near future."\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's linked exchange-rate regime was established in 1983, partly to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from panic selling amid escalating concerns about the former British colony's future, ahead of its handover to China in 1997.\par \par \uc2 The peg bolts Hong Kong's monetary policy to that of the U.S., meaning the city has had ultralow interest rates recently, spurring rising property prices because money is cheap to borrow for real-estate purchases.\par \par \uc2 That has led to calls for a review of the system, including from a former head of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Joseph Yam, who said in a research paper earlier this year that it was time for the government to look at options including a switch to pegging to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a complete float.\par \par \uc2 Frances Cheung, a senior strategist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2 , said, "The re-appreciation in the renminbi has given the Hong Kong dollar a further boost and led to some speculation on the dollar-peg regime."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 21, 2012 19:14 ET (23:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121021e8al000jj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
