\par\par \b \uc2 Paul Tudor Jones: US Can  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In HKD To Encourage Yuan Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Or   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2011\par 23:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Renowned hedge fund manager Paul Tudor Jones said an "easy policy tool" for the U.S. to push for a correction in the undervalued Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar.\par \par \uc2 The heart of Jones's argument, made in a Feb. 14 research paper sent by the founder of \uc2 Tudor Investment Corp\uc2 . to investors, is that the Hong Kong dollar and the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the renminbi, are now closely intertwined. Pushing up the value of the Hong Kong dollar would put pressure on Chinese authorities to increase the value of the renminbi, also known as the yuan. By \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the U.S. could "make a statement that would be highly symbolic if only slightly impactful."\par \par \uc2 Jones didn't address the political consequences that might result from such a dramatic policy.\par \par \uc2 Jones said it would be fairly inexpensive to push up the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. "Countervailing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar may not require a 10% premium to spot" exchange rate, he said. "Inevitably, the Hong Kong dollar is going to have to adjust to an appreciating renminbi and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this market is tantamount to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the RMB itself."\par \par \uc2 Unlike other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars trade in a narrow band around the rate of HK$7.8 to one U.S. dollar. If the exchange rate steers towards either end of the trading range, the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , backed by its U.S. dollar reserves, steps in to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jones said there is a sizeable pool of yuan in the city of Hong Kong. Citing a recent estimate by the head of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said there is approximately $50 billion in yuan circulating in Hong Kong and the number is growing by $10 billion per month.\par \par \uc2 Jones, a heavyweight fund manager and one of the most respected macro traders, added that the U.S. could also launch targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other non-Japanese Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help reduce the U.S.'s growing trade imbalances.\par \par \uc2 In the paper, he argued the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was highly undervalued. On a productivity-adjusted purchasing power parity basis, he said the renminbi was between 35% and 72% cheaper against the U.S. dollar in 2010 than in 1993.\par \par \uc2 As an alternate to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jones said the U.S. could state its desire for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate at least 10% a year in nominal terms for the next three years. The U.S. could during that time profit from investing in renminbi.\par \par \uc2 "It would be a good investment and could continue until RMB was at an appropriate level," he said.\par \par \uc2 Calling China's monetary policy one that is "drawn from textbooks that are centuries old," Jones said it is in China's and Asian nations' interest to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise.\par \par \uc2 "The recent popular uprisings in countries across North Africa are instructive because they are partly triggered by food price inflation," he wrote. "This may mark a turning point in how Asia views the continued merits of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suppression."\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds have a complex history in the region, dating back to the Asian financial crisis of the late 1990s. At the time, many politicians blamed hedge funds for making speculative trades on the rise and fall of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contributing to their collapse.\par \par \uc2 Then-Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad singled out billionaire investor George Soros in particular and famously called him a "moron."\par \par \uc2 -By Amy Or, Dow Jones Newswires; 212 416 3142; amy.or@dowjones.com [ 02-25-11 1726ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110225e72p000x2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
