\par\par \b \uc2 India Morning Call - Global Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 04:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2  ---------------------------(8:10\par a.m.)-------------------------\par  Stock Markets\par  DJIA          10,594.83  +22.10  Nikkei         9,580.56\par +71.06\par  NASDAQ         2,303.25   +1.93  FTSE          5,540.14\par -15.42\par  S&P 500        1,124.66   -0.41  Hang Seng     21,717.81\par +26.36\par  Bonds\par  US 10 YR Bond     2.763  +0.040\par  US 30 YR Bond     3.928  +0.051\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  EUR US$          1.3095  1.3098  Yen US$        85.73   85.75\par  Commodities\par  Gold (Lon)      1272.50          Silver (Lon)     20.760\par  Gold (NY)       1265.65          Light Crude       74.38\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overnight market action. Updates to Tokyo and Hong Kong.\f28 \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - U.S. stocks were little changed on Thursday as mixed economic data and a cautious forecast from economic bellwether \uc2 FedEx\uc2  kept the market locked in its recent tight trading range.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on [.N]\par \par \uc2  ---------\par \par \uc2  LONDON - Britain's top shares closed lower on Thursday, weighed by banks which slipped after recent strength, while retailers fell on downbeat UK economic data, countering gains in energy stocks.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on [.L]\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Japan's Nikkei average rose 0.8 percent on Friday, helped by a weaker yen after Japan's massive yen-selling this week, but gains were kept in check as investors watched if there would be further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on [.T]\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2   - Hong Kong stocks were set to open slightly higher on Friday and looked poised to post their third straight week of gains on the back of sustained strength in China's economy.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on [.HK]\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - The yen fell against the U.S. dollar on Thursday with the Bank of Japan silent after massive yen-selling in the prior session and investors reluctant to place bets in case the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins new sales to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on [USD/]\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  TREASURIES\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - U.S. Treasury prices fell on Thursday after mildly reassuring data reduced expectations the Federal Reserve would renew quantitative easing in the form of massive debt purchases aimed at jump-starting the economy.\par \par \uc2   For a full report, double click on [US/]\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  COMMODITIES\par \par \uc2  GOLD\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - Gold swept to all-time highs on Thursday for the second time this week, as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market jitters and broader economic uncertainty attracted more investors to the metal's safe-haven credentials.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on [GOL/]\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  BASE METALS\par \par \uc2  LONDON - Copper rose to a near two-week high on Thursday, lifted by a weaker dollar and fund buying, while an improvement in U.S. jobless data did little to soothe concerns about the strength of the economy.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on [MET/L]\par \par \uc2  - - - -\par \par \uc2  OIL\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - Oil prices fell nearly 2 percent on Thursday, sliding for a third day, as U.S. Midwest supply anxieties eased further on news that a major Canadian pipeline carrying crude to the region would be back in service by Friday.\par \par \uc2  (Compiled by Sunil Nair; Mumbai newsroom 91-22-66369036)   \par \par \uc2 INDIA-MORNING/|LANGEN|Table|SNI|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100917e69h00049\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
