\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/HKD Biased Up; 7.7750-7.7780 Band Tipped N/T\b0\par\par\uc2 1,475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 06:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD biased up as weakness in local stocks prompts investors to sell local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says trader at U.K. bank. HSI down 0.4%. Tips pair in 7.7750-7.7780 band near term. "The pair is very well-supported at 7.7750 as risk aversion remains the theme in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Some Chinese banks keep buying the U.S. unit," trader says. Pair last at 7.7767 vs 7.7760 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares ended higher, lifted primarily by sharp rebound of Ayala Land (ALI.PH), which appears to have been inadvertently sold down yesterday, traders say; benchmark PSEi closed 0.7% to 3554.15 in heavy volume even as losers trampled gainers, 96 to 38; Ayala Land rose nearly 16% to PHP16.80, erasing all its losses Tuesday, while PLDT (TEL.PH) gained 0.2% to PHP2,450; conglomerate SM Investments (SM.PH), the top traded stock, lost 1.2% to PHP484 while Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), down 1.7% to PHP64.20. "We will likely see the market consolidating in the coming days. The recent run up from 3400 straight to 3600 is too sharp. We really need a breather," says Joseph Roxas, president of Eagle Equities; PSEi expected to move within the 3500-3600 band in coming days. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.4% at 1400.58 midday on profit-taking after recent rally to 30-month highs. Losses in regional markets also weighing but dealers say after having gained for 8 straight sessions, KLCI's retreat today not surprising. "The market has never been able to stretch its gains in overbought territory since the bull market started in March 2009," OSK Research says. Dealers tip KLCI to extend declines near-term, targeting 1395-1400 range. Among blue chip decliners, Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) down 4.2% at MYR2.94, Axiata (6888.KU) down 1.3% at MYR4.44, MISC (3816.KU) down 1.7% at MYR8.75. Among gainers, Cocoaland (7205.KU) +5.1% at MYR2.90 on expectations company may soon sell shares to new strategic partner. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR lower but remains in tight range with no strong lead from stock market to help affirm clear direction, says state-run bank dealer. Pair last at 46.87 vs near 2-week high of 46.91 late Tuesday. Trader says routine USD selling by some custodial banks supporting INR but pair may recover later in session, touch resistance of 47.05 on possible oil-related buying. Tips support at 46.75. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei extends losses, off 1.4% at 8869.98, after Japan Finance Minister Noda lunch with Prime Minister Kan produces no major progress. Noda says he was told by Kan to watch markets carefully and adds that he was given no specific instructions on how to combat the sharp climb in the yen that is driving the market lower. "Investors have been let down every time hopes (of monetary easing) emerged, so it's very difficult for new funds to enter the market," says Investrust CEO Hiroyuki Fukunaga, noting low trading volume. Notes U.S. economic concerns also weighing on market ahead of release of durable goods orders and new home sales data later in global trading day. 30/33 Topix subindexes lower. Kyocera (6971.TO) down 1.4% at Y7,160, Fanuc (6954.TO) down 1.0% at Y9,160 on yen concerns. \uc2 Toyota Tsusho\uc2  (8015.TO) off 3.0% at Y1,201 after announcing secondary share offering. \uc2 Dainippon Sumitomo Pharma\uc2  (4506.TO) up 2.1% at Y694 on \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  rating upgrade. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moves lower, tracking EUR advance and aided by positive Dow Futures; pair last at 45.31 after settling at 45.53 late Tuesday following Monday's tragic hostage-taking of foreign tourists; support at 45.20, resistance 45.60. "The market's reaction to Monday's bloody incident appears to have also died down," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0411 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 2.4% at 423.89 -- at 15-month low -- with players selling blue chips across the board to meet margin calls, amid concerns that more investors will leave market this week as they lose faith in government which hasn't done anything to support equities. "As more people leave, I see stronger selling pressure to meet margin calls, pulling prices down further," says Hanoi-based trader. Adds buying sentiment expected to be weak because companies, banks planning to issue new equities worth more than VND30 trillion by year-end, causing fears of equity glut. Volume average with 51.5 million shares valued at VND1.25 trillion exchanging hands today. Among most actives, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends down 0.6% at VND15,600, REE (REE.VH) down 2.5% at VND15,500, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) down 4.9% at VND25,300. Traders tip index to drop below 420 Thursday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad higher on gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight while concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  possible rate tightening at September 16 policy review "not too worrisome", says senior trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.64 vs 98.62 last close, while most-traded 8.13% 2022 bond at 101.28 vs 101.15 last close. RBI in annual report Monday said, "despite the risk of a possible trade-off between growth and inflation in the short run, inflation containment may have to receive precedence." Trader says, market has priced in 25 basis point rate hike and with liquidity turning surplus lately, RBI statement may not induce panic. Adds, "besides, price pressures, especially food inflation, seems to be tapering off now." Tips 2020 bond in 98.50-98.70 band in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] LME copper getting some good dip-buying support from early China trade, which is positive for sentiment, but outlook later likely depends on economic data. "I think if the data continues to disappoint, we can head to $7,000 or $6,900," says Hong Kong based trader. Economic data later includes U.S. July durable goods orders at 1230 GMT and U.S. July new home sales at 1400 GMT. LME copper at $7,167.50/ton, up $31.50 with 1,540 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  tips 3-month Hibor to stay below 1% until late 2011 as Hong Kong rate hike unlikely until U.S. Federal Reserve tightens, expected in 2H of 2011. "Interest rates have remained at near record low level as global central banks maintain an accommodative stance, while the strong performance of the Chinese economy maintained investors' interest in Hong Kong dollar assets," prompting capital inflows, keeping interbank liquidity ample. 3-month Hibor last at 0.19% vs 0.20% yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex off 0.2% at 18,275.30, tracking weakness across global markets. Index breadth negative as 17 of 30 stocks lower, with losses led by metals, autos and financials. \uc2 HDFC Securities\uc2  tips support at 18,170. S&P CNX Nifty flat at 5505.35. "We reiterate the broad (Nifty) range for the week between 5440-5625," says Edelweiss Securities. However, says "heavyweights of the index are grossly underperforming, which is a cause of concern for the market sustenance." \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) down 1.2% at INR507.70, DLF (532868.BY) off 0.7% at INR320, \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY) down 0.4% at INR2,836, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) off 0.5% at INR1,005.05. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] Market watchers say momentum traders such as CTA funds and prop trading desks buying up JPY - testing to see if Bank of Japan will be able to step in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively. Tom Kaan of Louis Capital Markets in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  tips JPY rising to 80-81 level. Says markets always testing when confronted with weak environment; adds in the past, BOJ action ineffective. Also says, Asian central banks have also been pretty active with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , wouldn't be surprised to see banks diversifying out of USD and into JPY. "I don't see it ending" without concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by BOJ and/or U.S. Federal Reserve.(ellen.sheng@dowjones.com) [ 08-25-10 0041ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100825e68p0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
