\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/PHP Moves Lower, Tracking EUR; 45.20 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 06:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moves lower, tracking EUR advance and aided by positive Dow Futures; pair last at 45.31 after settling at 45.53 late Tuesday following Monday's tragic hostage-taking of foreign tourists; support at 45.20, resistance 45.60. "The market's reaction to Monday's bloody incident appears to have also died down," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0411 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 2.4% at 423.89 -- at 15-month low -- with players selling blue chips across the board to meet margin calls, amid concerns that more investors will leave market this week as they lose faith in government which hasn't done anything to support equities. "As more people leave, I see stronger selling pressure to meet margin calls, pulling prices down further," says Hanoi-based trader. Adds buying sentiment expected to be weak because companies, banks planning to issue new equities worth more than VND30 trillion by year-end, causing fears of equity glut. Volume average with 51.5 million shares valued at VND1.25 trillion exchanging hands today. Among most actives, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends down 0.6% at VND15,600, REE (REE.VH) down 2.5% at VND15,500, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) down 4.9% at VND25,300. Traders tip index to drop below 420 Thursday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad higher on gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight while concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  possible rate tightening at September 16 policy review "not too worrisome", says senior trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.64 vs 98.62 last close, while most-traded 8.13% 2022 bond at 101.28 vs 101.15 last close. RBI in annual report Monday said, "despite the risk of a possible trade-off between growth and inflation in the short run, inflation containment may have to receive precedence." Trader says, market has priced in 25 basis point rate hike and with liquidity turning surplus lately, RBI statement may not induce panic. Adds, "besides, price pressures, especially food inflation, seems to be tapering off now." Tips 2020 bond in 98.50-98.70 band in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] LME copper getting some good dip-buying support from early China trade, which is positive for sentiment, but outlook later likely depends on economic data. "I think if the data continues to disappoint, we can head to $7,000 or $6,900," says Hong Kong based trader. Economic data later includes U.S. July durable goods orders at 1230 GMT and U.S. July new home sales at 1400 GMT. LME copper at $7,167.50/ton, up $31.50 with 1,540 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  tips 3-month Hibor to stay below 1% until late 2011 as Hong Kong rate hike unlikely until U.S. Federal Reserve tightens, expected in 2H of 2011. "Interest rates have remained at near record low level as global central banks maintain an accommodative stance, while the strong performance of the Chinese economy maintained investors' interest in Hong Kong dollar assets," prompting capital inflows, keeping interbank liquidity ample. 3-month Hibor last at 0.19% vs 0.20% yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex off 0.2% at 18,275.30, tracking weakness across global markets. Index breadth negative as 17 of 30 stocks lower, with losses led by metals, autos and financials. \uc2 HDFC Securities\uc2  tips support at 18,170. S&P CNX Nifty flat at 5505.35. "We reiterate the broad (Nifty) range for the week between 5440-5625," says Edelweiss Securities. However, says "heavyweights of the index are grossly underperforming, which is a cause of concern for the market sustenance." \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) down 1.2% at INR507.70, DLF (532868.BY) off 0.7% at INR320, \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY) down 0.4% at INR2,836, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) off 0.5% at INR1,005.05. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] Market watchers say momentum traders such as CTA funds and prop trading desks buying up JPY - testing to see if Bank of Japan will be able to step in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively. Tom Kaan of Louis Capital Markets in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  tips JPY rising to 80-81 level. Says markets always testing when confronted with weak environment; adds in the past, BOJ action ineffective. Also says, Asian central banks have also been pretty active with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , wouldn't be surprised to see banks diversifying out of USD and into JPY. "I don't see it ending" without concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by BOJ and/or U.S. Federal Reserve.(ellen.sheng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses midday as banks, property developers and airlines fall. Shanghai Composite Index off 1.0% at 2623.94, tipped to stay in 2600-2700 range rest of session. "Agricultural Bank is back to its IPO price, indicating weak momentum in the banking sector and harming overall sentiment," says Zhu Haomin at Soochow Securities; adds investors still cautious amid Beijing's cleanup of local government financing, tight hold over property sector. Agricultural Bank of China (601288.SH) down 0.4% at CNY2.68, and \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) off 2.6% at CNY8.62. Airlines drop sharply in reaction to Henan Airlines' plane crash last night, though downside limited given strong 3Q earnings outlook. \uc2 Air China\uc2  (601111.SH) down 3.4% at CNY11.25, \uc2 China Eastern Airlines\uc2  (600115.SH) off 3.2% at CNY7.31. Shenzhen Composite Index off 1.2% at 2876.62. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY still higher midday in quiet trading, pair at 6.7990 vs 6.7972 last close, tipped to trade between 6.7970-6.8020 range. "Trading is still quiet for now, but demand for USD is expected to pick up a bit more later from companies," says Guangzhou-based foreign bank trader; adds yuan demand from hopes of stronger Chinese stock market yesterday has waned, with Shanghai Composite down 1.0%. Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6870/6.6900, up vs 6.6788/6.6828 late yesterday.(esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.4% at 3011.76, likely to remain weaker as continues to take lead from offshore markets, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Alexandra Dalzell; "This the most resounding influence at the moment, but in saying that, we're hurting less than the Australian, U.S. and European markets. So I guess what that says is there is enough buying interest to move it forward." Adds offshore weakness weighing on blue chips Telecom (TEL.NZ), down 1.5% at NZ$2.00, Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), down 0.3% at NZ$7.51. Says Michael Hill International (MHI.NZ) +6.2% at NZ$0.69 after Hill family said wanted to increase stake to 50.1% from 48.0%. Says \uc2 NZ Farming Systems Uruguay\uc2  (NZS.NZ) +1.4% at NZ$0.71 extending yesterday's 11.1% rise, after \uc2 Olam International\uc2  (O32.SG) raises bid for remaining shares in the company, topping offer from Union Agriculture Group; "There is nothing better than a trump offer to get the market going again." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold looks technically firmer after near vertical ascent back to over $1,230 level overnight on poor U.S. housing data; some participants also citing comments from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chief U.S. economist Jan Hatzius in Bloomberg TV interview that Federal Reserve likely to ease monetary policy further. Spot gold at $1,231/oz, up 60 cents since NY close. "The bounce sets up another leg higher on a break of last week's high of $1,237. We see $1,253 as the initial resistance from our channel top and $1,265 June (record) high," says ScotiaMocatta technical note. Mitsui Global Precious Metals says next week even more data intensive so players may position now. Next week includes U.S. August non-fram payrolls, ISM manufacturing, China PMI. "As we begin to move into September the potential exists for an indecisive market to take a more sustained direction, and for some significant money that has been sitting on the sidelines to come into play as we draw nearer the end of the year," says Mitsui. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds higher on buying by interbank players amid flush liquidity following persistent capital inflows. Some offshore players park funds in bonds after piling up THB positions during latest round of THB gains. Market largely priced in 25 bps hike at policy meeting this afternoon but focus on policy statement for potential trading cues. "It's not so common for yields to keep falling despite an anticipated rate hike. But this is what happens when there is such ample liquidity in the market," says bond trader. Profit-taking may emerge, keeping yields within 5 bps range in either direction rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.65%/2.62% vs 2.69%/2.65% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.72%/2.67% vs 2.81%/2.77%, June 2019 at 2.80%/2.75% vs 2.88%/2.86%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com) [ 08-25-10 0013ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100825e68p00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
