htens, expected in 2H of 2011. "Interest rates have remained at near record low level as global central banks maintain an accommodative stance, while the strong performance of the Chinese economy maintained investors' interest in Hong Kong dollar assets," prompting capital inflows, keeping interbank liquidity ample. 3-month Hibor last at 0.19% vs 0.20% yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex off 0.2% at 18,275.30, tracking weakness across global markets. Index breadth negative as 17 of 30 stocks lower, with losses led by metals, autos and financials. \uc2 HDFC Securities\uc2  tips support at 18,170. S&P CNX Nifty flat at 5505.35. "We reiterate the broad (Nifty) range for the week between 5440-5625," says Edelweiss Securities. However, says "heavyweights of the index are grossly underperforming, which is a cause of concern for the market sustenance." \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) down 1.2% at INR507.70, DLF (532868.BY) off 0.7% at INR320, \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY) down 0.4% at INR2,836, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) off 0.5% at INR1,005.05. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] Market watchers say momentum traders such as CTA funds and prop trading desks buying up JPY - testing to see if Bank of Japan will be able to step in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively. Tom Kaan of Louis Capital Markets in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  tips JPY rising to 80-81 level. Says markets always testing when confronted with weak environment; adds in the past, BOJ action ineffective. Also says, Asian central banks have also been pretty active with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , wouldn't be surprised to see banks diversifying out of USD and into JPY. "I don't see it ending" without concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by BOJ and/or U.S. Federal Reserve.(ellen.sheng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses midday as banks, property developers and airlines fall. Shanghai Composite Index off 1.0% at 2623.94, tipped to stay in 2600-2700 range rest of session. "Agricultural Bank is back to its IPO price, indicating weak momentum in the banking sector and harming overall sentiment," says Zhu Haomin at Soochow Securities; adds investors still cautious amid Beijing's cleanup of local government financing, tight hold over property sector. Agricultural Bank of China (601288.SH) down 0.4% at CNY2.68, and \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) off 2.6% at CNY8.62. Airlines drop sharply in reaction to Henan Airlines' plane crash last night, though downside limited given strong 3Q earnings outlook. \uc2 Air China\uc2  (601111.SH) down 3.4% at CNY11.25, \uc2 China Eastern Airlines\uc2  (600115.SH) off 3.2% at CNY7.31. Shenzhen Composite Index off 1.2% at 2876.62. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY still higher midday in quiet trading, pair at 6.7990 vs 6.7972 last close, tipped to trade between 6.7970-6.8020 range. "Trading is still quiet for now, but demand for USD is expected to pick up a bit more later from companies," says Guangzhou-based foreign bank trader; adds yuan demand from hopes of stronger Chinese stock market yesterday has waned, with Shanghai Composite down 1.0%. Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6870/6.6900, up vs 6.6788/6.6828 late yesterday.(esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.4% at 3011.76, likely to remain weaker as continues to take lead from offshore markets, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Alexandra Dalzell; "This the most resounding influence at the moment, but in saying that, we're hurting less than the Australian, U.S. and European markets. So I guess what that says is there is enough buying interest to move it forward." Adds offshore weakness weighing on blue chips Telecom (TEL.NZ), down 1.5% at NZ$2.00, Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), down 0.3% at NZ$7.51. Says Michael Hill International (MHI.NZ) +6.2% at NZ$0.69 after Hill family said wanted to increase stake to 50.1% from 48.0%. Says \uc2 NZ Farming Systems Uruguay\uc2  (NZS.NZ) +1.4% at NZ$0.71 extending yesterday's 11.1% rise, after \uc2 Olam International\uc2  (O32.SG) raises bid for remaining shares in the company, topping offer from Union Agriculture Group; "There is nothing better than a trump offer to get the market going again." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold looks technically firmer after near vertical ascent back to over $1,230 level overnight on poor U.S. housing data; some participants also citing comments from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chief U.S. economist Jan Hatzius in Bloomberg TV interview that Federal Reserve likely to ease monetary policy further. Spot gold at $1,231/oz, up 60 cents since NY close. "The bounce sets up another leg higher on a break of last week's high of $1,237. We see $1,253 as the initial resistance from our channel top and $1,265 June (record) high," says ScotiaMocatta technical note. Mitsui Global Precious Metals says next week even more data intensive so players may position now. Next week includes U.S. August non-fram payrolls, ISM manufacturing, China PMI. "As we begin to move into September the potential exists for an indecisive market to take a more sustained direction, and for some significant money that has been sitting on the sidelines to come into play as we draw nearer the end of the year," says Mitsui. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds higher on buying by interbank players amid flush liquidity following persistent capital inflows. Some offshore players park funds in bonds after piling up THB positions during latest round of THB gains. Market largely priced in 25 bps hike at policy meeting this afternoon but focus on policy statement for potential trading cues. "It's not so common for yields to keep falling despite an anticipated rate hike. But this is what happens when there is such ample liquidity in the market," says bond trader. Profit-taking may emerge, keeping yields within 5 bps range in either direction rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.65%/2.62% vs 2.69%/2.65% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.72%/2.67% vs 2.81%/2.77%, June 2019 at 2.80%/2.75% vs 2.88%/2.86%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0328 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tipped tad lower as worries over pace of U.S. economic recovery persist following drop in U.S. existing home sales, local equities may open negative, says private bank trader. Pair may open around 46.85 vs near two-week high of 46.91 late Tuesday. Adds, pair may gain later in month-end USD demand by oil companies, also rating downgrade on Ireland by one notch by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  may favor greenback. Tips pair in 46.70-47.00 band in session. Foreign funds were net buyers of provisional INR1.91 billion worth of Indian stocks on Tuesday, 2-month NDF quoting at 47.1325/47.17. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals up in early Asia trade after enduring a tough Tuesday as dismal U.S. housing data, stronger USD, 1,625-ton rise in LME copper warehouse stocks pushed prices lower. Metals helped by some dip buying from China, aided by EUR recovering some ground vs both JPY, USD. SHFE metals also only marginally lower -- Shanghai benchmark December copper down 0.4% at CNY56,710/ton -- despite much weaker LME overnight, which helping support LME prices. LME 3-month copper at $7,175/ton, up $39 with 1,448 lots done, at $54 premium to SHFE December contract. Economic data later includes U.S. July durable goods orders at 1230 GMT, U.S. July new home sales at 1400 GMT. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 08-25-10 0010ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100825e68p00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\pic