\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: China, Singapore Sign CNY150B  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Swap Agreement\b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2010\par 09:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap; other moves to encourage wider use of the yuan) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Aaron Back and David Roman \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--\uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  Friday signed a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap agreement with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore worth CNY150 billion, or S$30 billion, the latest in a series of moves by China to promote wider global use of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The agreement, which was signed in Beijing, is effective for three years, during which both central banks will be able to exchange their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from that of their counterpart.\par \par \uc2 The deal can also be extended beyond that period with the consent of both, the PBOC said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Over the last two years, China has signed a series of similar deals with countries including Indonesia, Malaysia, South Korea, Brazil, Belarus and Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say these have little effective value beyond the symbolic, as the yuan is not freely traded in financial markets and isn't widely used in business with China. But, they note the removal of some restrictions in recent months and efforts to boost yuan transactions in Hong Kong show Beijing is looking to raise the yuan's global profile, even as it keeps tight controls on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, China signed a deal that allows more yuan-denominated investment products to be offered in Hong Kong, including insurance policies and wealth-management plans, and lifts restrictions on some account transfers and the opening of accounts for corporate clients.\par \par \uc2 The deal also keeps other restrictions in place, including a CNY20,000 ($2,950) cap on daily yuan purchases for individuals, and rules that allow mainland regulators to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yuan transactions conducted in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the removal of these and other constraints, such as controls on cross-border flows, could add to already excessive liquidity and market bubbles in China, a key headache for Beijing policymakers.\par \par \uc2 -By Aaron Back and David Roman, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7701; aaron.back@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 23, 2010 03:35 ET (07:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100723e67n0012n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
