\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Wider Use Of Yuan A Long-Term Prospect\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2010\par 08:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details on China-Singapore deal announced Friday.)\par \par \uc2 By David Roman\par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Recent moves by China to encourage wider use of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overseas are unlikely to produce significant results for years due to major constraints on the use of the yuan, which remains hard to obtain outside the mainland.\par \par \uc2 This week, China signed an agreement with Singapore that allows the two countries' central banks to swap yuan for Singapore dollars, and another with Hong Kong removing some restrictions on yuan usage and circulation in Hong Kong. They're the latest moves in Beijing's campaign to boost use of the yuan as it seeks a more prominent international profile.\par \par \uc2 But those deals also showed that Chinese authorities are taking only baby steps for now and remain far from committed to the sort of radical changes needed to turn the yuan into a rival to the dollar. Such changes, such as allowing more freedom in cross-border flows and yuan usage for non-trade purposes, could add to already excessive liquidity and market bubbles in China, a key headache for Beijing policymakers, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong agreement, the most wide-ranging, allows more yuan-denominated investment products to be offered in Hong Kong, including insurance policies and wealth-management plans, and lifts restrictions on some account transfers and the opening of accounts for corporate clients. But many restrictions remain in place despite lobbying from Hong Kong's financial industry.\par \par \uc2 Among these, there's a CNY20,000 (US$2,950) cap on daily yuan purchases for individuals, and rules that allow mainland regulators to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yuan transactions conducted in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition, China is still dragging its feet on other key issues like allowing cross-border yuan transactions for financial investments, and the development of a market for yuan forwards that would make it easier to hedge deals in yuan.\par \par \uc2 These pending reforms may be hard to implement, said Kelvin Lau, a Hong Kong-based economist with Standard Chartered, one of several banks that plan to offer structured investment products in Hong Kong paying interest in yuan.\par \par \uc2 "China doesn't allow yuan to be channeled onshore. It needs to create more channels, but they're concerned about the effect of inflows," Lau said. "We do expect future liberalization steps to be taken on a quarterly, semiannual or yearly basis... one step at a time."\par \par \uc2 China's cautious pace in pushing the yuan, even in Hong Kong, has meant that measures taken until now have had little impact. Six years after the territory's residents were allowed to start keeping yuan deposits they remain a footnote for the island's economy, accounting for just over 1% of total deposits, roughly unchanged from a year ago. The U.S. dollar accounts for close to 30% of deposits.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't bode well for similar efforts outside of China. Previous "internationalization" efforts opened up trade invoicing in yuan to exporters and importers in 20 mainland provinces and around the world, but the impact has been muted due to strict controls on yuan convertibility that analysts blame on Beijing's concerns about hot-money inflows.\par \par \uc2 These controls limit the usefulness of the swap announced Friday with Singapore, which comes after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signed similar deals with countries ranging from Brazil to Belarus. But these have had mostly symbolic value so far as the yuan is not freely traded in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 In addition, companies that want to conduct trade with China in yuan must operate through just a handful of authorized banks, all based in mainland China, except for the \uc2 Bank of China (Hong Kong)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (David Roman, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 23-07-10 0655GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100723e67n000hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
