\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HK Short Sales Remain Low Midday; CMB Leads\b0\par\par\uc2 1,703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 08:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +11.7% at HK$1.336 billion, but as percentage of market total remains low at 4.3% vs 4.0% at Monday's half-day, indicating bears deterred by HSI stringing up 3-day winning streak. No stocks manage more than HK$100 million worth of shorts; \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Merchants Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (3968.HK) leads with HK$99.15 million, followed by China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$58.57 million. Then come Ping An (2318.HK) with HK$57.54 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$47.05 million, HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$42.92 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher with steel plays leading gains in Japan. Nikkei +1.0%, S&P/ASX +0.4%, NZX-50 flat, HSI +0.6%, STI flat, KLCI +0.1%, Kospi +0.3%, Taiex +0.1%, Sensex +0.1%, Shanghai Composite down 0.2%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR up on receding concerns around Greece's debt burden, with late gains vs JPY and USD also helped by Nikkei's solid uptick. EUR/JPY at 124.92 vs 124.65 late in NY Monday, EUR/USD higher at 1.3508 vs 1.3475, USD/JPY at 92.43 vs 92.49; raft of data from Japan, with output at factories and mines off seasonally adjusted 0.9% on-month in February vs +2.7% January; Japan February jobless rate at 4.9%, unchanged from January, in line with forecast in Dow Jones poll of economists, suggest labor market unlikely to improve much as economic environment still weak. Household spending down 0.5% on-year in February vs +1.5% expected, indicating consumption remains weak; production of cars, trucks and buses in Japan +74.9% on-year in February, while auto exports +79.8% on-year. Nymex May crude oil futures up 4 cents at $82.21/bbl. Spot gold at $1,111.50/oz, up $2.50 from NY close. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0605 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR rises vs USD, JPY as stronger equities -- Nikkei closes +1.0% -- prompt short-term players to buy risk-sensitive common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; "the recent calming of concerns over the Greek issue" also helping EUR, says Shinichi Hayashi, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But says as players remain hesitant to buy EUR aggressively before Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls report, first resistance for EUR/USD likely at 1.3530 vs last 1.3510. EUR/JPY last 124.93, further rises may be capped at 125.00. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.2% at fresh 9-week high of 7962.22, though trading volume down 5.8% vs yesterday's 7-week high, with non-tech names in demand. "The volume is still good, foreign institutional investors are on the buy side, and cross-strait trade pact talks will start tomorrow, which are all positive cues to the Taiex," says \uc2 Ta Ching Securities\uc2  manager Daniel Liu. Expects benchmark index to test 8000 Wednesday, while support eyed at 5-day moving average of 7888. Food and retail names shine; "The Chinese economy is going strong this year and the focus (of the government) is to boost domestic demand, so this will benefit Taiwan (food and retail) companies in China," says SinoPac Securities Investment Trust fund manager Harvey Chang. \uc2 Ruentex Industries\uc2  (2915.TW) ends +6.9% at 20-year high of NT$72.40, Uni-President Enterprises (1216.TW) +1.1% to NT$36.60, Far Eastern Department Stores (2903.TW) +0.8% to NT$26.75; Yuanta Financial (2885.TW) +0.5% to NT$19.55; SinoPac Financial (2890.TW) +3.2% to NT$11.35. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan key 10-year, 5-year government bonds end almost unchanged, as buying by banks mostly offset by players' profit-taking, says local bank trader; "both selling and buying are strong today," he says. Tips bonds to maintain consolidation mode tomorrow as players wait for cues from tomorrow's auction of NT$30 billion worth of 30-year reopened bonds, which tipped to be auctioned in 2.2000%-2.3000% band vs 2.2200% end today in when-issued trade. 10-year ends at 1.4390% vs 1.4399% yesterday, tipped in 1.4300%-1.4450% band tomorrow. 5-year yield ends at 1.0262% vs 1.0242% yesterday in when issued trade, tipped in 1.0100%-1.0300% band tomorrow. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0537 GMT [Dow Jones] India overnight call rates, collateralized borrowing and lending obligation or CBLO up on borrowing by private banks ahead of fiscal year ending, says dealer with state run bank; call rates at 4.85% on negotiated dealing system vs 3.90% Monday's close; CBLO now at 4.60% vs 4.00% at open. "While state run banks remain flush with funds it is the private banks which are borrowing. With the fiscal year ending tomorrow, banks would like to keep ample liquidity on books," says dealer. Tips call rates to remain in 4.00%-4.25% range while CBLO to move down to 3.50% in session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.0% at 778.77 at midday pause in relatively moderate trade. Rebound after 3 straight sessions of falls not yet strong enough to break through 780 resistance. "The volume is not supportive for the breakthrough although the external cues are positive today," says Thanchart Securities' strategist Pichai Lertsupongkit. Adds, market not surprised by failed talks between government, anti-government protesters. Support put at 770. Among top actives, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) +0.9% at THB28.25, \uc2 Thanachart Capital\uc2  (TCAP.TH) +5.8% at THB25.50, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.6% at THB251, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +1.1% at THB145. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  India says country's rising stock market is supported by stronger INR vs USD and ongoing net foreign inflows into equities, but adds, while the rally could last a little longer, "we expect profit-taking, as the P/E ratio for Sensex is now expensive at 17X estimated FY11 EPS (analysts' earnings upgrades are possible after June)." House says most positives in stock prices and uncertainties over monsoon season and farm growth not priced in yet; recommends stocks with earnings growth, strong balance sheets and P/E ratios below 10X. Tips top picks as \uc2 Sterlite Industries\uc2  (500900.BY), \uc2 Hindalco Industries\uc2  (500440.BY), \uc2 Dalmia Cement\uc2  (500097.BY), \uc2 Mphasis\uc2  (562299.BY) and Titagarh Wagon (532966.BY). Sensex last +0.1% at 17,731.44, around levels last seen in February 2008. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] Macquarie says recently only tactically oriented investors driven by fear of missing short-term catch-up moves by stock market (mostly foreigners) are now fading away; notes monthly turnover for foreign fund flows in value terms is now down around 60% since 2007 highs, despite increased volumes from new TSE Arrowhead trading system. Foreigner share in total turnover is down from 63% in FY ending March 2008 to 54% for current FY to date. "We believe the Japan 'catch-up' trade ended in the second week of January," after Topix had risen 20% in seven weeks. Says foreign buying may be expected to fade away, barring profound (investment) policy change. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher in afternoon trade, recovering tad from steep downward correction seen in recent sessions including yesterday, analysts say. "If the 3-year yield happens to shoot above the key 4.00% level, if and when bonds fall again, the overall bullish trend for bonds may be reversed; otherwise, any yield gains will likely be perceived as good opportunities to grab bonds on dips," says IBK Securities analyst Ough Chang-sup. Adds, 12-month leading average numbers from February industrial output data due Wednesday likely to support view that local economic recovery momentum losing steam, support bond sentiment. Tips 3-year yield in 3.85%-3.95% range for today, last down 4 bps at 3.88%, 5-year yield quoted down 2 bps at 4.51%, June futures +6 ticks at 110.61. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.1% midday at 2798.008 in moderate volume; gains in select banking, small-cap stocks keeping benchmark index in positive territory, traders say. Tip 2780-2820 range for rest of day. Still, index off earlier high of 2817.708; "profit-taking by most local funds has taken place after the main index failed to test the resistance level of 2820," says trader at First Asia Capital. Among gainers, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +1.9% at IDR5,500, Bank Tabungan (BBTN.JK) +2.2% at IDR1,310. Car maker Astra(ASII.JK) down 0.8% at IDR42,300 on profit-taking, while nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) down 1.1% at IDR4,650. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD down at 7.7637 vs 7.7645 late yesterday on inflows, trader from U.K. bank says. "Given HKMA by default doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) unless USD/HKD hits 7.75, some funds still prefer \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  as many Asian central banks actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at whatever level they wish." HK rates remain low due to flush liquidity, benchmark 3-month Hibor at 0.10% vs 0.09% late yesterday; local swap rates largely flat across sectors, 1-year HKD IRS flat at 0.40%, 2-year down 1 bp at 1.00%, 5-year flat at 2.27%, 10-year unchanged at 3.24%. (aries.poon@dowjones.com) [ 03-30-10 0231ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100330e63u0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
