\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/HKD Down On Inflows, Rates Largely Flat\b0\par\par\uc2 1,761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2010\par 07:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD down at 7.7637 vs 7.7645 late yesterday on inflows, trader from U.K. bank says. "Given HKMA by default doesn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market) unless USD/HKD hits 7.75, some funds still prefer \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as many Asian central banks actively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at whatever level they wish." HK rates remain low due to flush liquidity, benchmark 3-month Hibor at 0.10% vs 0.09% late yesterday; local swap rates largely flat across sectors, 1-year HKD IRS flat at 0.40%, 2-year down 1 bp at 1.00%, 5-year flat at 2.27%, 10-year unchanged at 3.24%. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1320.00, off morning high of 1323.83 as gains from construction (subindex +0.5%) and plantations (+0.3%) more than offset losses in technology, properties and industrial products. "PM Najib's announcements on the new economic model didn't provide much impetus as they were not entirely unexpected," says dealer; tips 1313-1323 range with downside bias on disappointment over lack of details on new economic model. Market breadth negative with losers outnumbering gainers 391 to 191. Among gainers, Pos (4634.KU) +9.7% at MYR2.26 after PM Najib says Khazanah will sell its controlling 32% stake in post office operator, \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +1.7% at MYR16.72, MRCB (1651.KU) +10% at MYR1.65 on expectation it may win bid to develop land to be tendered out by government and JCY (5161.KU) + 2.8% at MYR2.88. On downside, Proton slipped 2.7% to MYR4.64, Shell (4324.KU) off 0.7% at MYR10.72 and Notion (0083.KU) down 2.6% at MYR3.40.(elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.3% at 1696.96 in light volume led by banks, but off early high of 1705.57 on continued stock selling by local funds. "Foreigners extended their buying spree for the 13th straight day, which is good. But local funds continued to cash in profits due to rising fund redemptions after the Kospi reached 1700," says Chung Myoung-ji at \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2 . Still, expects uptrend to remain intact, foreigners to continue to buy local stocks, citing expectations for strong 1Q earnings of local firms, discounts of local stocks' valuation to global peers. Expects Kospi to test this year's high around 1720 later this week after U.S. economic data release. Expectations for 1Q earnings surprise, potentially active M&As among local banks this year lifting banks, says Chung; \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) +6.7% at KRW16,800, \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE) +4.2% at KRW34,600. \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +0.8% at KRW26,050, Hynix (000660.SE) +3.5% at KRW26,750 on hopes for earnings surprise. But \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.2% at KRW816,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.3% at KRW115,500 on program selling. (soo-kyung.seo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: South Korea's consumer prices likely increased 0.6% on month in March vs +0.4% in February, due to higher global oil prices and as abnormally cold weather conditions boosted local fresh food costs, shows median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones; would be biggest on-month gain since +0.7% in March 2009. On year, prices likely +2.6% vs +2.7% in February. Consumer prices growing above 3%, center of BOK's target range of 2%-4% for this year, unlikely in coming months, but "inflation will likely accelerate to mid-3% from the end of the third quarter when the recovery gathers further momentum," says Lee Sang-jae at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 . Data due 0430 GMT Thursday. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.6% at 21,365.28 midday, supported by DJIA gains overnight, but any further rise in local index likely capped at month-to-date peak of 21,440 says Louis Tse at Value Convergence CEF. "Volume remains modest (HK$30.74 billion midday) and I don't expect gains to go beyond the 21,500 mark this week." Expects buying momentum, on window dressing, to sustain at least up to tomorrow ahead of 1st quarter-end. Expects more volatility in 2Q, as concerns over potential rate hike in China may weigh on mainland bank and property stocks. Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +0.8% at HK$102.20, \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) +0.4% at HK$58.50, with their results just released during lunch break. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's prime minister Najib Razak unveils New Economic Model (NEM) that targets annual GDP growth of 6.5% from 2011 to 2020. Najib says new plan to lift country into high income-status, expects GDP per capita to rise to $15,000-$20,000 by 2020 from currently below $7000. Says aggregate demand will have to grow at a robust pace, investment (particularly private sector) to be main driver: private investment to form 20% of 2020 GDP by 2020 vs 10% in 2010. Target fiscal deficit to a near balance by 2020 from 7.4% of GDP in 2009. CIMB chief economist Lee Heng Guie says NEM is "strong statement of intent, bold." However, details are still lacking, depends on implementation. Thinks "growth target is challenging given the uncertain external economic environment." (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, underpinned by quarter-end buying of blue chips amid overnight advance of overseas equities; bellwether PSEi nudged 0.8% to 26-month high 3202.17; volume heavy on several block sales involving shares of power distributor \uc2 Manila Electric Co\uc2 . (MER.PH). "Overall sentiment is still positive as there were no negative news in both economic and corporate fronts," notes Lawrence de Leon, trader with Accord Capital Equities, adding market likely to advance further Wednesday on expected follow through buying ahead of Easter holiday; next resistance 3300, support 3100. Leading market advance are \uc2 Ayala Land Inc\uc2 .(ALI.PH) up 3.8% to PHP13.50; \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH), up 2.3% to PHP330.00; \uc2 First Philippine Holdings Corp\uc2 . (FPH.PH) up 1.8% to PHP56.50.(rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares pull back from early highs as players prefer to book gains rather than stay invested amid lack of incentives. STI flat at 2929.20 midday vs high 2936.40 (+0.2%); support expected at 2900, resistance at January high of 2947. Market breadth flat vs 2.5 gainers for every decliner in early trade. Participation remains low with merely 722.6 million shares changing hands, still driven mainly by penny stocks. "Share prices are not coming down much and there's still money flowing around, so there's still room for the market to go up," says broker at local house. Among blue chips, banks and developers mixed, plantation and rig-building stocks up, telcos down. \uc2 Neptune Orient Lines\uc2  (N03.SG) best percentage gainer, +2.6% at S$1.98, on optimism freight rates will continue to improve. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0428 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.6% at 11,057.02, slightly up from morning close of 11,038.08, with 30/33 Topix subindexes higher. Iron & steel (+2.2%), pulp & paper (+2.6%) sectors continue to lead market, with wholesale traders (+2.2%) also supported on higher commodities prices. Nikkei may be top heavy at its current level, says Shoji Yoshigoe, general manager at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities, citing some short-term indicators showing markets becoming overheated. But adds, index could still gain another 50 points given futures-led buying. \uc2 JFE Holdings\uc2  (5411.TO) up 2.7% at Y3,795, \uc2 Nippon Oil\uc2  (5001.TO) up 3.1% at Y953, \uc2 Nippon Paper\uc2  (3893.TO) up 3.1% at Y2,415, \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2  (8058.TO) +3.8% at Y2,461. Topix up 0.9% at 974.45. (atsuko.fukase@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls below 31.800 psychological support; pair last at 31.773 vs 31.815 late yesterday, hits lowest level in nearly two weeks on quarter-end export settlements. Also, foreign fund inflows continue on the back of strong local stocks (Taiex last +0.2%) ahead of second round of talks on broad cross-strait trade agreement with China starting Wednesday, says local bank trader. Tips support at 31.750 as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to buy late in session to stem TWD's rise, help exporters. "The trade talk and exporter selling will keep the U.S. dollar weak for a couple of days," he says. Tips USD/TWD in 31.750-31.800 band in afternoon trade. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY slightly down midday as little-changed parity weigh on buying interest given there's little room for speculators to make money; pair at 6.8261 vs yesterday's OTC close of 6.8264. "Trade volume is thin today. With the central parity being so stable, I don't expect spot rate to move much, as people can't make money out of buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  onshore," says Shenzhen-based trader with local bank; tips pair to trade in 6.8250-6.8270 range for rest of session. Central parity set at 6.8264, hardly budging from yesterday's 6.8266. (michelle.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 1.0% at 500.72 as institutional investors sold to boost cash funds by end-1Q. "Though the government today announced the economy expanded 5.83% on year in 1Q, players understand that they can buy shares at lower prices as funds were trying to sell," says chief analyst Le Anh Thi of Au Viet Securities. Volume heavy at 59.9 million shares traded worth VND2.24 trillion. Among actives, Sacom (SAM.VH) ends +0.6% at VND45,000, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) down 1.4% at VND21,800, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) down 3.3% at VND43,500. Tips key index in 495-505 range tomorrow. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 03-30-10 0113ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100330e63u0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
