\par\par \b \uc2 HK Dollar Up Late; To Rise Less Than Other Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2010\par 09:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                           Latest     Change \par USD/HKD Spot              7.7549     -0.0003 \par 1-year USD/HKD Forward*   -214       -4 \par Overnight Hibor           0.08%      -18 bps \par 1-month Hibor             0.06%      -20 bps \par 3-month Hibor             0.12%      Unchanged \par 1-year Hibor              0.48%      +13 bps \par 2-year EF Note            0.47%      -1 bp \par 10-year EF Note           2.90%      -7 bps \par  \par *mid-point of bid-offer spread \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar continued to rise against the U.S. dollar late Tuesday, because China's stronger-than-expected exports in December encouraged funds to switch out of the safe-haven but low-yielding U.S. unit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 But the Hong Kong dollar is likely to gain less than most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term because of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's passive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and strong local corporate demand for the U.S. dollar at the beginning of a year, traders said.\par \par \uc2 In late Asian trade, the U.S. dollar was at HK$7.7549, down from HK$7.7552 late Monday. The U.S. unit was fixed at HK$7.7547 earlier Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 China's exports surged 17.7% in December from a year earlier, much bigger than the 5% gain expected by economists and ending 13 months of contraction.\par \par \uc2 "The Hong Kong dollar will likely be less volatile relative to other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Singapore and Taiwan dollar, because the latest inflows to Asia seem to be drawn to markets where central banks actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a senior trader at a local bank said.\par \par \uc2 Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the Hong Kong dollar is allowed to trade between HK$7.75 and HK$7.85 to the U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when the U.S. dollar trades outside the range.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar will likely find strong support at HK$7.7530 this week, the trader said.\par \par \uc2 Calyon economist Sebastien Barbe said inflows to Asia have increased since last week, particularly to India, South Korea, Taiwan and Thailand, where central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they deem it appropriate.\par \par \uc2 "In the near term, the U.S. dollar (against the Hong Kong dollar) may remain relatively supported given seasonal corporate demand. But beyond January, we see limited reason for the U.S. dollar to move away from HK$7.7500," another trader at a U.K. bank said.\par \par \uc2 The one-year U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar forward contract was quoted at a discount of 214 points to the spot rate, compared with a 210-point discount late Monday.\par \par \uc2 -By Aries Poon, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2832-2332; aries.poon@dowjones.com [ 12-01-10 0818GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1053 4178   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100112e61c000nl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
