\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asian Reserves Down In Dec, Hit By Stronger Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2010\par 05:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell for the first time in 10 months, hit by a strong rise in the U.S. dollar that lowered the value of non-greenback holdings, but may resume their climb in coming months.\par \par \uc2 According to calculations by Dow Jones Newswires, reserves held by 11 key Asian central banks, excluding China's, stood at US$2.753 trillion at the end of December, down from US$2.783 trillion at the end of November, a fall of 1.1% on the month.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied in December against the main alternative reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which together account for around 30% of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The dollar rose 7.6% against the Japanese yen, 4.8% against the euro and 1.8% against the British pound in December.\par \par \uc2 But the dollar was flat or lower for the month against key Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a trend that analysts expect to continue this year, given strong economic growth and foreign inflows anticipated across East and South Asia. Many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain shy of the highs they hit against the dollar just before the financial crisis exploded in mid-2008, which should keep reserves across the region on the upswing in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 China, which releases its data on a quarterly basis, is expected to provide a reserves update later this week. Last week, state-run Shanghai Securities News said reserves at the end of 2009 may have exceeded US$2.4 trillion, citing an unnamed source at a state-run big bank. That compares with US$2.273 at the end of September.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the expected continuation of strong inflows to Asia will be the main reason for increases in Asian reserves this year.\par \par \uc2 Sebastian Barbe, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Calyon, noted the inflows are a consequence of the region's persistent current account surpluses, which will continue to exert appreciation pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To respond to this, most Asian central banks - wary of how strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  affect local exporters - will keep a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars, Barbe added.\par \par \uc2 Even in December, as the dollar rose against the likes of the euro and yen, several Asian central banks, including in South Korea and Taiwan, were suspected of buying dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the rise of their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  story is part of the balance-of-payments story," Barbe said.\par \par \uc2 Still, any increase in reserves in 2010 is likely to be slower than last year's frantic pace. Excluding China, reserves were 14% higher in December from the year earlier, with whopping increases of 40% seen in Hong Kong, 33% in South Korea and 28% in Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, a stronger dollar and weaker valuation of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves should slow reserve accumulation considerably. That's especially the case in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for most of the reserve increase last year but is expected to fall dramatically as the local dollar, which is pegged to the greenback, weakens on expectations of coming U.S. interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Following are the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for Asia's 12 leading economies. All numbers are in billions of U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 The total number excludes China.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par              Dec 09       Dec 08      Nov 09    As Of \par TOTAL         2,753        2,405       2,783   end Dec Ex China \par  \par China         2,273        1,946       2,273   end Sep \par Japan         1,049        1,003       1,074   end Dec \par India         283.5        254.6       286.7     1-Jan \par Indonesia      66.1         51.6        65.8   end Dec \par Korea           267        201.2       270.9   end Dec \par Malaysia       96.7         91.4        96.1   end Dec \par Pakistan         15          9.7        13.7     2-Jan \par Philippines      45         37.1        43.7   end Dec \par Singapore     187.8        174.2       188.9   end Dec \par Taiwan        348.2        291.7       347.2   end Dec \par Thailand      138.4        110.9       139.3    30-Dec \par Hong Kong     255.8        182.5       256.3   end Dec \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Roman, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 12-01-10 0443GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \pa