\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Hibor-Libor Spread To Make Life Easier For HKMA\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2009\par 07:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar's drop from very strong levels against the U.S. dollar reduces the pressure on the territory's long-standing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg and should make life easier for the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The slump in the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to nine-month lows this week is very much a U.S. dollar story, and probably marks the end of a year of Hong Kong dollar strength. Supported by a growing view the Federal Reserve may start raising interest rates around midyear, the greenback could rise in coming months toward HK$7.8000, the midpoint of the HKMA's band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair, from HK$7.7566 now.\par \par \uc2 Higher policy rates in the U.S. imply an increase in interbank rates, used by commercial banks. This would encourage investors to borrow Hong Kong dollars and buy U.S. dollars, to capture the widening spread between Hong Kong's Hibor rates and U.S. dollar Libor rates.\par \par \uc2 The HKMA keeps the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged in its policy band against the U.S. dollar and moves in lockstep with the Fed on policy rates, but the Hibor-Libor spread fluctuates, widening when rates generally are rising because Hong Kong interbank rates rarely rise as fast as Libor. The benchmark three-month Hibor is usually 10 to 100 basis points below Libor, reflecting the low interbank risk in wealthy, bank-friendly Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The spread has been a fairly reliable indicator of Hong Kong dollar performance in recent years: the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at times been weak despite rallies in Hong Kong stocks and gained even as stocks fell back.\par \par \uc2 The spread narrowed to almost zero this year and stayed at 5 to 10 basis points most of the second half as the Fed vowed to keep rates extremely low for quite some time. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained stuck at the bottom of the HKMA's band, with the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling over HK$640 billion to defend the peg since October last year.\par \par \uc2 The Fed Wednesday kept its key rate target at zero to 0.25% and repeated this will stay low for "an extended period," but was more upbeat about the economy, prompting speculation it's preparing the way for rate hikes. The Hibor-Libor spread has inched up to 14 basis points and looks set to widen further, boosting the greenback against the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2 Federal-fund futures now price a 36% chance of a Fed hike to 0.5% by June--twice the probability implied just two weeks ago. This shift should boost the three-month Hibor from its recent record low of 0.25%, and the pace of the rise is likely to dictate the greenback's rally against the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2 Libor rates are bottoming just as Hibors are falling on a large increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar liquidity from the HKMA's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months. Monetary base stands at HK$312.4 billion, 30 times higher than 18 months ago.\par \par \uc2 The Hibor-Libor spread will keep widening as long as this excess liquidity remains in the system. There's no incentive for Hong Kong banks to push interbank rates higher until the HKMA begins to mop up the extra cash.\par \par \uc2 (David Roman, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporter, has been a Dow Jones Newswires correspondent since 2002, covering economic and corporate news. He can be reached at 65 6415 4045 or by e-mail: david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 18-12-09 0632GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1171 5008 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091218e5ci000am\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
