\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-HK "very concerned" about asset bubble risk\b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2009\par 00:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Hong Kong govt says no property bubble apparent yet\par \par \uc2 * No suggestion of further measures to control prices\par \par \uc2 * Echoes concerns of other emerging markets\par \par \uc2 * Industry executives see little threat of bubble so far (Adds details, industry quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Susan Fenton\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Dec 7 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's government is "very concerned" about the risk of an asset bubble developing although one is not apparent yet, Financial Secretary John Tsang said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Tsang did not point to any potential measures to combat the city's main concern which is property prices, but his remarks reflect worries in many emerging markets over a flood of cash flowing into their economies seeking higher returns.\par \par \uc2 Both Brazil and Taiwan have imposed capital controls to try to limit the inflows and policymakers elsewhere are talking about potential measures they could take. [ID:nLJ437424]\par \par \uc2 "We are very concerned about the risk of an asset bubble," Tsang told legislators. "The risk is there but it is not very apparent."\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, residential property prices have jumped 30 percent this year and the luxury sector is up more than 40 percent after the city drew a record $73 billion in capital inflows between October 2008 and Nov. 13, 2009.\par \par \uc2 Tsang said the financial system was sound and the city could cope with capital inflows and outflows, but he echoed similar concerns voiced by Hong Kong Chief Executive Donald Tsang and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Norman Chan.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong, like other economies in Asia, is watching warily for the formation of asset bubbles to avoid compromising nascent economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recently reduced the maximum mortgage for luxury properties to 60 percent from 70 percent of the price in an effort to cool the market, and mortgage demand has eased from a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 But the HKMA is more tied than many central banks because the Hong Kong dollar's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar means it has to adopt Federal Reserve monetary policy. U.S. rates have been held between zero and 0.25 percent since December.\par \par \uc2 The inflow of capital is putting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar under pressure and prompting the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its tight trade band.\par \par \uc2 Some industry executives in Hong Kong said there was little threat of prices bubbles in the property sector because surges had been limited to certain sectors and particular regions.\par \par \uc2 "We cannot completely rule out that relatively severe bubbles are forming in some parts of Asia-Pacific," Leung Chun-ying, Asia-Pacific chairman of property agency DTZ, told a news conference.\par \par \uc2 "But in general, I think the Asia property market, including Hong Kong, doesn't have any problems of bubbles forming for now," he said.\par \par \uc2 A government economist told legislators that speculation in the property market was not too heated and that demand was mainly user-oriented, with 90 percent of transactions for mass-market property. Housing affordability meanwhile was above a 20-year average. (Additional reporting by Lee Chyen Yee; Editing by Chris Lewis and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 HONGKONG/TSANG (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091207e5c70002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
