\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD:Hong Kong Snaps Up Treasurys To Defend Dollar Peg\b0\par\par\uc2 By Min Zeng   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2009\par 19:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong, the world's seventh-largest holder of foreign reserves, has become one of the biggest buyers of Treasury securities outside the U.S. this year as local officials stepped up efforts to defend the 26-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is part of China but administered separately, reflects the challenge facing policy makers in many Asian economies: managing the influx of investments, fueled by cheap U.S. dollars, while at the same time preventing their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating sharply and hurting their export industries, for many of these countries the key driver of growth.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's close links with China make it particularly attractive to foreign investors, but other export-driven Asian economies--from Indonesia to Thailand--have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating sharply.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar's weakness has forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or HKMA, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March, selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sometimes several times a day to maintain the peg at a rate of HK$7.8 to the U.S. dollar. The local dollar is allowed to trade between HK$7.75 and HK$7.85 to the U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been aggressive: the HKMA sold HK$9.68 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, after selling HK$2.33 billion Monday.\par \par \uc2 As a result, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of Hong Kong have risen to $240.1 billion at the end of October from $182.5 billion at the end of last year, mostly held in U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 "All central banks will buy U.S. assets when they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Steven Major, global head of fixed-income research at \uc2 HSBC Holdings Plc\uc2  in London. That's to prevent the additional liquidity from exacerbating already rising asset prices.\par \par \uc2 The HKMA, Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bought $55 billion in U.S. Treasurys this year through the end of September, accounting for about 13.7% of net purchases by foreign investors and 23.8% of net buying by foreign central banks, according to data compiled by Dow Jones Newswires based on records from the Treasury Department.\par \par \uc2 In the same period, China, the largest foreign holder of U.S. Treasurys, purchased $71.5 billion in Treasurys and Japan, the second-largest holder, bought $125.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 More than 90% of Hong Kong's purchases this year was in Treasury bills, whose maturities are one year or less.\par \par \uc2 Parking cash in the most liquid part of the Treasury market gives the Hong Kong authorities more flexibility should capital suddenly flow out of the territory if sentiment on global economic growth turns sour, said Rachel Ziemba, an analyst in New York at Roubini Global Economics who closely monitors foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities in the Treasurys market.\par \par \uc2 Along with demand from hedge funds, banks and mutual funds to improve their balance sheets ahead of the year end, buying by foreign central banks has helped keep Treasury yields at relatively low levels. That's kept funding costs down at a time when the U.S. government recently reported a budget deficit around $1.4 trillion in the fiscal year 2009 that ended in September.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, as the U.S. dollar fell sharply amid expectations that U.S. interest rates will remain low even as the global economic recovery gathers pace, the Hong Kong dollar was trading around HK$7.7501 against its U.S. counterpart.\par \par \uc2 HKMA Chief Executive Norman Chan last week reiterated that the government has no plans to alter the Hong Kong dollar peg to the U.S. dollar, saying that it has helped to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability.\par \par \uc2 (Min Zeng, a reporter on the Treasury desk, writes about global government bond markets for Dow Jones Newswires. He can be reached at 212-416-2229 or via email at: min.zeng@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 25-11-09 1855GMT ]\par \par \uc2 70718   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091125e5bp001f6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
