\par\par \b \uc2 HK Dollar Slightly Down Late; HKMA Expects Inflows To Continue\b0\par\par\uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2009\par 09:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                           Latest     Change \par USD/HKD Spot              7.7502     +0.0001 \par 1-year USD/HKD Forward*   -243       +5 \par Overnight Hibor           0.02%      -6 bps \par 1-month Hibor             0.10%      +1 bp \par 3-month Hibor             0.15%      Unchanged \par 1-year Hibor              0.34%      -24 bps \par 2-year EF Note            0.45%      +9 bps \par 10-year EF Note           2.19%      Unchanged \par  \par *mid-point of bid-offer spread \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 HONG KONG\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (Dow Jones)--The latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  temporarily curbed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar's strength, leading the unit slightly lower against the U.S. dollar late Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 But the HKMA said inflows to Hong Kong will continue in the near term, keeping the local unit near the strong end of its trading band against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The HKMA sold HK$4.65 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market earlier Friday when the U.S. dollar hit HK$7.7500. In late Asian trade, the U.S. dollar was at HK$7.7502, compared with HK$7.7501 late Thursday. The U.S. unit was fixed at HK$7.7501 earlier Friday.\par \par \uc2 Inflows to Hong Kong have been strong since the second quarter, mainly because of the economic recovery in Hong Kong and China, the HKMA said in a document presented to the Legislative Council on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "With upbeat economic growth in China and renewed appreciation pressure on renminbi (the Chinese yuan), investment demand for Hong Kong dollar (denominated) assets is likely to remain strong," the HKMA said.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said strong inflows "may (also) give rise to inflation in asset prices and consumer prices, which could pose threats to macroeconomic stability."\par \par \uc2 A trader at a Chinese bank said: "Capital inflows (to Hong Kong) are being boosted by renewed expectation of a stronger yuan."\par \par \uc2 Those expectations came after the People's Bank of China said in its quarterly monetary policy report issued this week that it will take into account "changes in international capital flows and the trends of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in exchange-rate policy, a phrase it hadn't used previously.\par \par \uc2 The one-year U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar forward contract was quoted at a discount of 243 points to the spot rate, compared with a 248-point discount late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The HKMA said in the document a discount of around 200 points "suggests little speculation on the Hong Kong dollar exchange rate." It didn't elaborate.\par \par \uc2 A strategist at a U.K. bank said: "Market talk of Hong Kong dollar peg changes are hyperbole and highly unlikely."\par \par \uc2 Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the Hong Kong dollar is allowed to trade between HK$7.75 and HK$7.85 to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 -By Aries Poon, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2832-2332; aries.poon@dowjones.com [ 13-11-09 0810GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1053 4178   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091113e5bd000y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
