\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Kangaroos Court Australian Bond Investors - Heard\b0\par\par\uc2 1,337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2009\par 08:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's banks might have to look abroad more often to issue debt; great news for foreign companies thinking of selling bonds Down Under. Foreigners have sold US$12.3 billion worth of kangaroo bonds this year, up 36% from 2008, Dealogic says. Australia's US$1 trillion pension pot is one draw. Recently, though, cross \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps rates have jumped, making terms for those looking to repatriate funds from Australia back to their home country better than any point on record. This comes amid expectation that Aussie banks will have to go abroad, if regulators limit how much of each other's debt the banks can hold. See HEARD ON THE STREET column: (MHX)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] Better-than-expected industrial output growth in September not sign of "broad-based recovery" in economy, says Rupa Rege Nitsure, chief economist at \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2 . "Non-food credit growth continues to be sluggish. We cannot call a broad-based recovery till exports recover." Expects RBI to raise cash reserve ratio only in Jan-March quarter, followed by rise in rates only in April-June. India's industrial output +9.1% on-year in September, after revised 11.0% increase in August, vs +7% tipped by 15 economists in Dow Jones Newswires poll. (SDS)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI unlikely to hurry into rate hikes despite strong industrial output data, say economists after September factory output grew 9.1% from year-earlier, outpacing median +7% estimate of 15 analysts polled by Dow Jones. "There are linkages between rural economy and manufacturing activities and following the late monsoon the rural economy is hurt," says Sachchidanand Shukla, chief economist at Enam Securities; adds, RBI likely to pause and watch how macro economic factors play out before rushing into rate hike. "We expect industrial output figure to remain strong through the second half of this (fiscal) year, but that will also be due to a weak comparable base," says Samiran Chakraborty, head of research at Standard Chartered Bank. (ABG)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] September industrial output data beat expectations but it may be too early to call a trend, says Namrata Padhye, economist at IDBI Gilts. "Monthly industrial output numbers tend to be volatile. We need to wait at least 2-3 months before we can see a clear trend," says Padhye. Notes, while signs of recovery have emerged in recent months, this month's data not in itself sufficient to advance RBI tightening. India's industrial output +9.1% on-year in September, after revised 11.0% increase in August, showed government data issued earlier today. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY, EUR/USD edge lower on profit-taking, but EUR/JPY may rise to 135.30, EUR/USD to 1.5040 if U.S. weekly jobless claims due at 1330 GMT come in better-than-expected, says Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager Yuzo Sakai; "Market sentiment is improving, helped by a rising trend in commodity markets," so any positive surprises may buoy risk appetite further. Dow Jones poll tips weekly jobless claims to fall to 510,000 in week ended Nov. 7 from 512,000 in week earlier. EUR/JPY last at 134.81; EUR/USD at 1.4999. (MIN)\par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD remains lower in afternoon session, down at 32.282 vs 32.310 late yesterday, on foreign funds inflows, export settlements, but trading volume thin so far, says local bank trader. "There's going to be heavy selling by exporters above 32.300," he says. Tips pair in tight 32.270-32.300 band for rest of session. (ABP)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.9% at 22,419.76, on profit-taking after earlier setting fresh 15-month intraday high of 22,707.36 and after 4-session, 5.3% rally. Taifook says with valuation of broad market reaching normalized levels, stock picking becomes "a key challenge" for aggressive investors. "M&A or related speculation has been a major catalyst for stock outperformance of late, with Wednesday's highfliers providing further proof." \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  (0023.HK) +2.1% at HK$34.05, trimming gains with intraday high at HK$36.50, after bank says it isn't aware of any reasons for jump in its share price (stock +15.2% Wednesday on M&A speculation). HKEx (0388.HK) down 1.7% at HK$139.00, as 3Q results (released yesterday midday) fail to impress. Volume brisk at HK$46.34 billion.(SUT)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones]Indian government bonds slip on higher than expected industrial production print, 6.90% 2019 bond at 96.86 vs 97.03 pre-data, 97.02 last close. "With the economy showing signs of having recovered, RBI tightening is becoming an increasingly active concern," says state-run bank dealer. Tips further rise in yield of 10-year benchmark by 2-3 bps. September industrial output grew 9.1% on year vs 7% median estimate in Dow Jones poll of 15 economists. (BIJ)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD steady as funds inflows to \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to continue, triggering more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by HKMA to curb HKD's gain, says senior trader at local bank. Pair flat at 7.7501. "Also, some market participants considered the change of wording in PBOC's quarterly monetary report as a sign that China may allow yuan to appreciate again. It will encourage more capital inflows as the freely convertible Hong Kong dollar is often traded as a proxy for the yuan," trader says. Tips pair in 7.7500-7.7505 in coming week. (YKS)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +6.6% midday at HK$1.229 billion, but as percentage of market total unchanged vs Wednesday's half-day at 3.0%, suggesting view HSI retreat merely due to profit-taking, further downside may not be big. HKEx (0388.HK) tops list with HK$198.32 million, only stock to draw more than HK$100 million. BYD (1211.HK) in second place with HK$66.66 million, followed by mainland's biggest 3 banks: ICBC (1398.HK) with HK$52.59 million, Bank of China (3988.HK) HK$42.65 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) HK$40.69 million. (RLI)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia CDS tightens further as risk appetite improves, but Calyon analyst Brayan Lai warns market may be underpicing risks amid plentiful liquidity. "The CDS bias in the immediate near-term would appear to be wider. The reality is there is no circumvention around society's desire for cheap readily available credit." Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade CDS index tightens to around 103/104 bps vs 105.75/109.25 bps late yesterday. Single-name CDS largely steady; Korea CDS are 2 bps tighter at 93/95 vs late yesterday after BOK keeps its policy rate steady at 2.00% earlier today, with governor signaling \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to hike rate by year-end. (ARP)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's 38 chief economists at private research houses expect on average Japan's CPI to fall 1.61% in Oct-Dec quarter (2.3% drop logged in Jul-Sep), with falling price trend to last until 1st quarter 2011, shows ESP Forecast Survey, monthly survey conducted by government-affiliated body. Result largely in line with BOJ's price view; shows top analysts in Japan think BOJ won't hike rates for a while, which is positive for JGB market but negative for JPY. Data also show half of surveyed analysts expect JGB market may suffer downward pressure if Japan ends up issuing more than Y50 trillion of bonds in fiscal 2010 starting April, while one-forth of surveyed said Y45 trillion worth of selling enough to push JGBs down.(TMO) [ 11-12-09 0209ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091112e5bc0004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
