\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HKMA Oct  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Set To Be Highest This Yr\b0\par\par\uc2 1,330 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2009\par 09:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] October is set to become month of heaviest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market by HKMA so far this year, as inflows to Asia have been exceptionally strong this month, traders and analysts say. Notes HKMA sells total HK$6.59 billion today in two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg, bringing total amount to HK$71.25 billion this month so far, nearing May's figure, when HKMA sold a total HK$73.78 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. "The local property and stocks market are still holding firm; there is no incentive to push investors out of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," says trader at U.K. bank; more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by HKMA look imminent. USD/HKD last at 7.7501 vs 7.7502 yesterday. USD/HKD pegged in 7.7500-7.8500 band. (YKS)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] The GBP's recent rally may be over but it may take until Nov. 5 for the pound to fall back again. The reason? Continued confusion over what the Bank of England intends to do with quantitative easing, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus by Nicholas Hastings. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 ("MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Will Be "Sticky To The Upside"," at 0711 GMT, misstated the previous close of the USD/JPY exchange rate. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY could prove a difficult play as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts to give up its recent gains. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  notes USD/JPY will be "sticky to the upside" as USD remains under siege from a rise in risk appetite. "We prefer going JPY-short against the EUR, AUD and NZD," the bank says. USD/JPY at 91.79 from the previous close of 91.28. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares likely to end higher, although upside from current levels may be limited given few leads to stoke more buying. STI +1.1% at 2712.24, not expected to breach year-to-date high of 2739. Gainers outnumbering decliners by 3 to 1 in overall market, but volume remains light. "The market looks strong but there's not much volume to speak about," says dealer at foreign brokerage. With earnings season underway, AmFraser Securities' head of retail research Najeeb Jarhom says certain sectors may offer trading opportunities; "a cursory glance suggests property could potentially be a star performer in this current earnings season if rallies in key plays led by Capitaland, City and \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  are any indication." \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  (C31.SG) +1.7% at S$4.27, CityDev (C09.SG) +1.6% at S$10.20, \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG) +1.8% at S$2.82. (FKH)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY could prove a difficult play as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts to give up its recent gains. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  notes USD/JPY will be "sticky to the upside" as USD remains under siege from a rise in risk appetite. "We prefer going JPY-short against the EUR, AUD and NZD," the bank says. USD/JPY at 91.79 from the previous close of 991.28. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR tumbles under 9,500 support after positive sovereign ratings comments by S&P, Pimco in quick succession. Pair now at 9,455 vs 9,540 last close; was stuck around 9,490 prior to ratings news. Dealers say convincing dip below 9,450 support could spark run for 9,400. S&P ups sovereign outlook to positive from stable, affirms BB- long-term foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign rating, BB+ L/T local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on expectation debt reduction, cautious fiscal management to continue. Separately, Pimco vice president Chia-Liang Lian tips Indonesia to regain investment-grade status in 3-5 years; sees similarities with China, India, says the three countries should be referred to as "ICI." "The market likes this," \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer says. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.6% at 1267.48, gains led by finance, select construction and shares of government-linked companies. Still, benchmark index off intraday high of 1269.14.; volume traded remains modest with 412.1 million shares worth MYR559.5 million while market breadth turns negative with decliners pipping gainers 303 to 288. Dealer says profit-taking picking up pace "ahead of the 2010 Budget announcement." Tips benchmark to stay in 1260-1270 range. "In line with the strength from the regional bourses, we expect markets to end on a positive note today. Traders should however be cautious of profit-taking with the impending weekend," says CIMB. Among gainers, Genting (3182.KU) +0.5% at MYR7.74, Gamuda (5398.KU) +1.2% at MYR3.34, CIMB (1023.KU) +1.1% at MYR12.70. On downside, Axiata (688.KU) down 1.6% at MYR3.0, Supermax (7106.KU) down 1.0% at MYR3.91 and \uc2 IGB Corp\uc2  (1597.KU) down 1.9% at MYR2.10. (VGB)\par \par \uc2 0702 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  raises outlook on Indonesia sovereign rating to positive from stable, affirms BB- long-term foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign rating, BB+ L/T local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating. Says change takes into account expectation "that debt reduction and underlying cautious fiscal management will remain key elements of macroeconomic policy. It also incorporates our view that a reform-minded leadership with a fresh and increased mandate will continue to pursue microeconomic reforms to increase Indonesia's growth potential, and further strengthen policy coordination and implementation of thorough administrative reforms." Upgrade comes shortly after President Yudhoyono says 7% growth a reality by 2014. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0702 GMT [Dow Jones] An expected pick up in US home sales Friday as well as gains in the euro zone's PMI and Germany's Ifo survey should boost risk-taking and weigh even further on the USD later in the day, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . But, the bank warns, "some profit-taking may still emerge at the end of an intense week." The USD is starting the day off a little higher against the EUR, at $1.5001. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] A return of risk appetite, encouraged by higher equity prices, left the dollar mixed at the opening in Europe Friday. However, the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did manage to rise to Y91.87 from Y91.28 while the euro slipped to $1.5001 from $1.5031. The euro was up, however, at Y137.82 from Y137.20. In equities, the 1.3% rise in the Dow Jones Industrial Average was followed by a 0.2% increase in the Nikkei and a sharp 2.1% gain in the Shanghai Composite Index. Improved sentiment about the global recovery also lifted the price of crude oil, with the price of contracts on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  rising another 14 cents from New York's close to trade at $81.33 a barrel. (NEH)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds fall below 120.95 Friday, confirming the negative bias after the recent bout of range trading, says Charles Diebel, Nomura strategist. Further losses will target a fall to 120.50 and 120.00 while a recovery will see the bund targeting 122.00. December bunds down 0.35 at 120.84 (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD lower, with Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , other players selling good amounts, as record $123 billion U.S. note supply next week expected to push U.S. rates higher, narrowing rate gap with EU, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major European bank. EUR/USD last at 1.5001, with next support eyed at 1.4950. Says hedge-funds, other short-term players also continuing to sell EUR/USD to lock in profits, after pair hit fresh 14-month high earlier in day at 1.5061. Adds, tone for European trade likely set by EUR-zone October PMI and German October Ifo at 0800 GMT, followed by U.K 3Q GDP at 0830 GMT. (ATM) [ 10-23-09 0324ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091023e5an0004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
