\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yuan NDFs scale back appreciation, HK dlr fwds wider\b0\par\par\uc2 876 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2009\par 08:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2  * Yuan NDFs off lows, but price in 3.5 percent gains in year\par \par \uc2  * Hong Kong dollar forwards move on risk of revaluation\par \par \uc2  By Vidya Ranganathan\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Chinese yuan non-deliverable forwards rose on Wednesday, showing a slight scaling back of market expectations for appreciation of the yuan, while Hong Kong dollar forwards moved to price in the risk of a revaluation of that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell, with the Korean won the worst hit, as the dollar <.DXY> rallied against commodity and high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and after Brazil's decision to impose a tax on foreign investment led to some nervousness among emerging market investors.\par \par \uc2  CHINESE YUAN\par \par \uc2  Chinese yuan NDFs were off Tuesday's lows, at which level they had priced in more than 4.5 percent of appreciation within 12 months -- a bullishness last seen in August 2008.\par \par \uc2  Yet, they still priced in 3.5 percent of appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the next 12 months from the spot rate of 6.827. Much of that has been spurred by foreign capital inflows into Chinese assets and punts on an economy that is recovering rapidly from the crisis, albeit aided by government stimulus.\par \par \uc2  Traders said some investors might be getting carried away with speculating on yuan gains, given it has been tightly held for months and has not appreciated at all so far this year.\par \par \uc2  "For you to be short NDFs, the negative carry is insane. So it only means they are expecting something out of the ordinary, like a revaluation of something," said one NDF trader in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  Onshore yuan forwards <CNYXFWD=CFXS> have fallen to 6.7 in the one-year tenor over the past week from 6.75 per dollar, as hedge funds and traders locked in the arbitrage between NDF and onshore. The onshore one-year yuan is still way cheaper than NDF, where it is quoted at 6.6 per dollar. Traders said that had limited the drop in NDFs.\par \par \uc2  "I suspect CNY will be at least 4 percent stronger a year from now but I doubt too many of the yuan buyers in NDFs are buy-and-hold types, in which case they're probably premature," said Sean Callow, a strategist at Westpac Bank.\par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't be chasing it myself since I get the impression that the ultimate decision makers in China are still inclined to go full throttle on fiscal, monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy i.e loose fiscal, no rate hikes, falling yuan NEER," Callow said.\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG FORWARDS\par \par \uc2  Hong Kong dollar forwards in the nine months to one-year tenors moved in step with recent movements in yuan NDFs, pricing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a higher premium to the U.S. dollar and thereby implying a risk to the peg.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hugging the top of its 7.75-7.85 per U.S. dollar band since June, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to defend the band.\par \par \uc2  The growing speculation of yuan appreciation appeared to have further boosted expectations either of the Hong Kong dollar being pegged at a stronger level or being pegged to the yuan.\par \par \uc2  "The rise in yuan appreciation expectation also appears to be spilling into market interests on a Hong Kong dollar move, as option-related interests on downside U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar emerge," analysts at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2  "However, we retain the expectation for the peg to remain in place."\par \par \uc2  Hong Kong dollar 9-month forwards show the U.S. dollar at a 165 pips discount, compared with 125 pips a week ago. The options market showed options to be long the Hong Kong dollar <HKDVOL=GFI> were at a premium to those being long U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Change on the day at   0531 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        90.70          90.67       -0.03\par  Sing dlr        1.3937         1.3953       +0.11\par  Taiwan dlr      32.364         32.275       -0.27\par  Korean won     1178.00        1165.90       -1.03\par  Baht             33.39          33.39       +0.00\par  Peso             46.97          46.76       -0.45\par  Rupiah         9440.00        9390.00       -0.53\par  Rupee            46.44          46.11       -0.70\par  Ringgit         3.3850         3.3620       -0.68\par  Yuan            6.8271         6.8265       -0.01\par  Change so far in 2009\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        90.70          90.60       -0.11\par  Sing dlr        1.3937         1.4340       +2.89\par  Taiwan dlr      32.364         32.860       +1.53\par  Korean won     1178.00        1259.50       +6.92\par  Baht             33.39          34.80       +4.22\par  Peso             46.97          47.52       +1.17\par  Rupiah         9440.00       11000.00      +16.53\par  Rupee            46.44          48.71       +4.90\par  Ringgit         3.3850         3.4500       +1.92\par  Yuan            6.8271         6.8230       -0.06\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  (Reporting by Vidya Ranganathan; Editing by Kazunori\par Takada) ((vidya.ranganathan@thomsonreuters.com; +65-68703090;\par Reu