\par\par \b \uc2 HK Dlr Flat Late; Continued Inflows Offset Broad US Dlr Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2009\par 10:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                           Latest     Change \par USD/HKD Spot              7.7501     Unchanged \par 1-year USD/HKD Forward*   -188       -2 \par Overnight Hibor           0.26%      +25 bps \par 1-month Hibor             0.26%      +20 bps \par 3-month Hibor             0.18%      +2 bps \par 1-year Hibor              0.71%      -8 bps \par 2-year EF Note            0.47%      -2 bps \par 10-year EF Note           2.19%      -3 bps \par  \par *mid-point of bid-offer spread \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar was steady against the U.S. dollar late Monday, as continued capital inflows to Hong Kong offset the effect of the U.S. unit's rebound on international markets Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Traders said they expect the Hong Kong dollar to remain near the upper limit of its the trading band against the U.S. dollar until at least the end of the month as gains in local shares continue to lure funds to the city. They said they expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a tight range of between HK$7.7500 and HK$7.7505 this week.\par \par \uc2 In late Asian trade, the U.S. dollar was at HK$7.7501, unchanged from late Friday. The U.S. unit was fixed at HK$7.7502 earlier Monday.\par \par \uc2 "It seems there was a slight correction in the commodities and U.S. stock markets, prompting investors to take a breather from buying higher return assets, which helped support the U.S. dollar overseas and locally," said a senior trader at a local bank.\par \par \uc2 "Still, the boost to the U.S. dollar was only mild and the main theme of a weak U.S. dollar hasn't changed."\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 0.67% Friday at 9995.91.\par \par \uc2 The euro dropped to US$1.4885 late Friday from US$1.4920 late Thursday, while the U.S. dollar rose to Y90.87 from Y90.70.\par \par \uc2 Another trader at a U.S. bank said he expects more market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, as capital inflows are likely to persist while local stocks are buoyant.\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Hang Seng Index ended at a more than 14-month high Monday of 22,200.46, up 1.2% on the day, boosted by gains on China's stock markets.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the HKMA sold HK$41.85 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to defend the peg. Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the Hong Kong dollar is permitted to trade only between HK$7.75 and HK$7.85 to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The one-year U.S. dollar/Hong Kong contract was quoted at a discount of 188 points to the spot rate, compared with a 186-point discount late Friday.\par \par \uc2 -By Chester Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; chester.yung@dowjones.com [ 19-10-09 0816GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1053 4178   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020091019e5aj000nq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
