\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ CHINA VIEW: Dollar Weakness Sends Ripples Across Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 By Craig Stephen  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN  \par \uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2009\par 14:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 The fall in the U.S. dollar to 14-month lows is generating increased media coverage in recent days and ratcheting up political tensions.\par \par \uc2 U.S. policy on the dollar is a particularly sensitive topic in Asia, as where the dollar goes, so too does the yuan and the Hong Kong dollar. This makes finding ways to preserve capital in a time of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness a priority.\par \par \uc2 This \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relationship between the U.S. and China has long been a source of periodic tensions. We are used to hearing the Chinese complaining about the declining dollar eroding its mountain of greenback reserves, while at the same time the gripe of the U.S. is that the yuan is undervalued. Now, the decline in the dollar is only dragging the yuan lower with it.\par \par \uc2 After allowing the yuan to appreciate by roughly 20% against the dollar from mid-2005, Beijing put the brakes on that movement when export growth contracted.\par \par \uc2 At least the silver lining for China of this new bout of dollar weakness is that its exports will get a much-needed boost, particularly in Europe and Japan, where "Made in China" products are now looking that much cheaper.\par \par \uc2 At a time when everyone would like to hold their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  low to support flagging exports, it hasn't gone unnoticed that the Chinese manufacturing giant has seen a competitive devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Last week it was reported that central banks around Asia, including Thailand, Malaysia, Taiwan and Singapore, were \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars. These countries will be wary of letting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate against the U.S. dollar (and hence, the yuan) and making their exports less competitive.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong also is acutely sensitive to the movements in the dollar, thanks to its 26-year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Hong Kong's new monetary chief Norman Chan did not quite get a baptism of fire, but was given warning his tenure at the helm of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be all calm. After marking his first day in office the previous week with a reiteration of his support for the dollar peg, he was then forced to follow words with action. HKMA needed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice Thursday to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar within its trading band, injecting HK$8.525 billion into the money market in total.\par \par \uc2 Typically the weak U.S. dollar makes Hong Kong assets attractive, and funds pour into the territory.\par \par \uc2 As the financial system gets swamped by fund flows, money market rates have been driven to the floor. This is all good for owners of property or equities, who tend to gain from asset inflation, although worries exist that these conditions are setting up a new bubble.\par \par \uc2 For many, however, the downside is the dwindling purchasing power of their hard-earned Hong Kong dollars, amid the nearing of 0% interest rates on deposit.\par \par \uc2 What's hard to swallow is that the puny state of the Hong Kong dollar is not down to the government here running a gargantuan deficit or a blown-out financial system, but just to the peg. This comes at a time when regional neighbors in Asia, and even those across the border in mainland China, are seeing their purchasing power increase.\par \par \uc2 The only thing left to do is position for capital preservation. Gold and the Aussie and New Zealand dollars are all becoming favored plays, once again. And the Hong Kong dollar is becoming the new carry trade, due to the plentiful and cheap financing, while the dollar peg makes it a one-way bet.\par \par \uc2 This is all good as long as the HKMA keeps its promise of not changing the rules of the game and announcing a wider peg or new dollar valuation. The consensus is that there will be no change until China makes its capital account fully convertible, which should be years away.\par \par \uc2 But China might have to act faster in the event we do get a full-blown dollar crisis leading to much higher interest rates in the U.S. Then there would be little advantage of being attached to the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Another dynamic we are beginning to see involves countries benefiting from strong inflows of funds needing to raise rates to curb growth. Last week for instance, Australia raised interest rates. This could accelerate current \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, as investors go after the yield advantage. We have seen this cycle before, and for now it looks as if it will repeat.\par \par \uc2 The best advice is to watch announcements on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy carefully. And that's not just the U.S. policy on the dollar, but any hint from mainland Chinese officials of what they plan for the yuan and the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Craig Stephen is a freelancer for MarketWatch. He can be reached at 415-439-6400 or via email at AskNewswires@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 12, 2009 08:02 ET (12:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020091012e5ac0025c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
