\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia shares hit 14-mth peak, dollar slumps\b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2009\par 06:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Asia shares hit 14-mth highs after strong U.S. retail sales\par \par \uc2 * Rising risk appetite sends U.S. dollar to 14-month low\par \par \uc2 * Oil rises to one-year high near $76\par \par \uc2 * Aussie, NZ \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  jump as rate rise speculation grows\par \par \uc2 * Korea, Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Susan Fenton\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Oct 15 (Reuters) - Asian shares rose to 14-month highs on Thursday as upbeat retail sales and surprisingly bullish earnings reports in the United States buoyed risk appetite, pushing the U.S. dollar to a 14-month low.\par \par \uc2 Shares in Japan jumped more than 2 percent as investors bought exporters who might benefit from rising U.S. demand and stocks in the rest of Asia firmed to their highest levels since last August.\par \par \uc2 Positive sales and earnings data from the United States -- including from \uc2 Intel Corp\uc2  and \uc2 JP Morgan Chase & Co\uc2  -- pushed the Dow Jones up 1.5 percent on Wednesday and above 10,000 points for the first time in a year.\par \par \uc2 U.S. government data showed that retail sales, excluding autos, rose for a second straight month in September.\par \par \uc2 Growing confidence that the United States can fuel a global economic recovery encouraged investors to buy riskier assets, such as higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Australian and New Zealand dollars, which were additionally boosted by rising expectations for interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Taiwan, Korea and the Philippines were spotted buying U.S. dollars to curb strength in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Several Asia countries have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks to cap their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep them competitive in export markets.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie dollar hit a 14-month high at $0.9183 to the U.S. dollar after the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  pointed to more rate rises. Last week, it became the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Group of 20 to tighten policy as the global financial crisis eases.\par \par \uc2 "We have said that, over time, interest rates will need to be adjusted towards a more normal setting as the economy recovers," \uc2 Governor Glenn Stevens\uc2  said in a speech. "A step in that direction was taken last week."\par \par \uc2 In New Zealand, data showing stronger-than-expected inflation raised the likelihood of a rise in interest rates in coming months and sent the Kiwi to a 15-month high of $0.7485.\par \par \uc2 The flow of cash into riskier assets has knocked the U.S. dollar, a trend that pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 14-month low of 75.284 against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and against the euro at $1.4958.\par \par \uc2 Expectations of healthy global growth prodded NYMEX crude futures to a one-year high near $76 a barrel after U.S. industry data showed a surprise drop in inventories.\par \par \uc2 Gold gold held above $1,060 an ounce, just below Wednesday's record high above $1,070.\par \par \uc2 JAPAN EXPORTERS GAIN\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's Nikkei average rose 2.1 percent to 10,272.62, helped by auto maker \uc2 Honda Motor Co\uc2 , which gained 2.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI index of Asia Pacific stocks traded outside Japan rose 1.4 percent to 414.96, its highest level since August last year. The Thomson Reuters index of regional shares was up 0.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Shares in Hong Kong rose 1.6 percent and those in Shanghai increased 0.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Bullish U.S. earnings reports boosted technology shares in Taiwan, lifting Taiwan's benchmark stock index by nearly 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Acer Inc\uc2 , which surpassed \uc2 Dell Inc\uc2  in the third quarter to become the world's No. 2 PC maker behind \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2 , rose 1 percent. (Additional reporting by Gyles Beckford in WELLINGTON and Anirban Nag in SYDNEY; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL (WRAPUP 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA|SXNA    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020091012e5ac0006r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
