\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA LOCAL BONDS-Malaysian, Thai curves flatten on rate views\b0\par\par\uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2009\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Sept 2 (Reuters) - The Malaysian yield curve extended a two-week flattening trend on Wednesday, with strong demand for new benchmark five-year debt helping sustain a decline in longer-end yields.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's bond yield curve also saw spreads tightening on views the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep its rate unchanged until early next year to spur the domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 For intraday updates, click on [ASIA-EMRG-FIXI]\par \par \uc2 VIEWS AND FLOWS\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's yield curve has flattened over the past two weeks, with the spread between the three-year and five-year bonds falling to 85.8 basis points (bps) on Wednesday from 98.7 on Aug 14. The flattening has been led by a drop in 5-year debt yields.\par \par \uc2 "Economic recovery is not yet very certain in Malaysia, so demand for government bonds continues. We have seen strong interest on the new five-year benchmark. Being a new issue, it is very liquid," a Kuala Lumpur-based trader said.\par \par \uc2 Even before the government sold the the 5-year debt on Aug. 26, investors had been buying the bond in a forward market, traders said.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, investors had been avoiding the short end of the curve, leading to the flattening.\par \par \uc2 The risk of a sell-off in three-year debt on views the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easing cycle has ended, and supply concerns at the 10-year tenor were also driving investors toward the five-year security, said Suresh Kumar Ramanathan, rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CIMB Investment Bank.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its rate on hold for a fourth straight meeting on Aug. 25, after cutting it by a total of 150 bps between November and April.\par \par \uc2 Traders expect the flattening trend to be sustained, on expectations the five-year yield would extend its fall.\par \par \uc2 THAI YIELD CURVE FLAT\par \par \uc2 The Thai yield curve has bear-flattened since last week, led by a rise in short-end yields as the market priced in steady policy rates until the first half of 2010 from a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has already paused policy for a while.\par \par \uc2 The spread between one-year and five-year bond yields fell to 148.5 bps on Wednesday from 182 on Aug. 24. The flattening was led by a rise in the one-year yield.\par \par \uc2 "The short-end of the curve, particularly the one-year, is being sold off because of expectations that monetary policy will remain unchanged for the rest of the year and maybe until early next year," a trader from Bangkok said.\par \par \uc2 "So there is no incentive to hold on to the one-year sector because of the limited upside."\par \par \uc2 At its last meeting on Aug. 26, the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  kept its rates unchanged at 1.25 percent for the third meeting in a row, after aggressive cuts from December to April totalling 250 bps.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Philippine five-year bonds <PH5YT=RR> extended a steep fall to take their yield up 7 bps to 6.38 percent. Traders said investors were selling the maturity in anticipation of a new retail Treasury bond.\par \par \uc2 The government is in advanced talks with banks to manage the retail issue and is considering maturities of 3, 5, and 7 years. [ID:nMNB002456]\par \par \uc2 A two-year bond sale in Hong Kong was hugely oversubscribed, reflecting high liquidity in the banking system following \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar.\par \par \uc2 The government sold HK$3.5 billion of the debt, with total tenders at HK$22.584 billion.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jun Ebias; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-BOND|LANGEN|ABN|D|SI|Z|PHD|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090902e592000pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
