\par\par \b \uc2 HK's Yam does not see  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  speculation, asset bubble\b0\par\par\uc2 By Susan Fenton   \par \uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2009\par 10:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Aug 6 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Joseph Yam said on Thursday that strong fund flows into Hong Kong assets in recent months, which have pushed the Hong Kong dollar <HKD=> to the top of its trading band, are not related to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation.\par \par \uc2 Funds have poured into Hong Kong assets -- pushing stock prices up 84 percent since a global equity rebound began on March 9, while property prices are up about 20 percent this year -- but Yam said he did not see any signs yet of excessive credit creation that could lead to an asset price bubble.\par \par \uc2 "Judging by information from our market contacts, these inflows were largely 'real money' -- that is, not related to any speculative activity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- from investors attracted by the opportunities here and looking to position themselves to benefit from the strong outlook for the mainland (China) and Hong Kong economies," Yam wrote in a weekly column on the bank's website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.info.gov.hk/hkma/eng/viewpt/index.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.info.gov.hk/hkma/eng/viewpt/index.htm}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition, a narrowing of the differential between U.S. and Hong Kong interest rates had discouraged selling Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars and put further upward pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Yam said.\par \par \uc2 "While we do not see any signs yet of excessive credit creation, which might fuel speculation in asset markets, we are mindful of the potential negative impact that asset-price bubbles, if they were to occur, could have on financial stability and especially the stability of the banking system," he said.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong has no control over monetary policy because its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar, forcing it to track U.S. monetary policy. However, Yam said the city could introduce supervisory measures to ensure that banks did not expand their lending to asset markets imprudently.\par \par \uc2 "This would help restrain credit expansion arising from strong capital inflows, and banks' credit-risk exposure, which might increase sharply if an asset-price bubble were to burst," he said.\par \par \uc2 Yam also warned that capital flows could reverse if economic conditions or corporate earnings disappoint, if U.S. interest rates rise -- Hong Kong rates would follow suit -- or, more positively, if investment opportunities overseas improved.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) has had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly since March, selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  for U.S. dollars, to keep the Hong Kong dollar within its fixed trading band with the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts say the territory's asset markets are at risk of forming a bubble -- excessive price appreciation that will result in a sharp correction -- over the next year.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong is seen as attractive because its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reasonably stable thanks to its peg to the U.S. dollar and because it gives exposure to China, one of few major economies that is likely to see significant growth this year. (Reporting by Susan Fenton, Editing by Chris Lewis)\par \par \uc2 HONGKONG/YAM (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|ABX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090806e586000ta\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
