\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: HK Shares End At Another 10-Month High\b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2009\par 12:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("HK Shares End At Another 10-Mo High Led By China Mobile,Cnooc," published at 0944 GMT, misstated Sichuan Expressway's gain on its trading debut in the 10th paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Gains in heavyweight China Mobile and oil giant Cnooc on hopes they will list on the mainland soon led Hong Kong shares to another 10-month closing high Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Hang Seng Index rose 372.92 points, or 1.84%, to 20,624.54, its highest closing level since Sept. 8. The index traded between 20,664.48 and 20,109.55 during the session.\par \par \uc2 Turnover totaled HK$84.84 billion, up from HK$80.90 billion Monday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect a pullback in the market after July futures expire Thursday, with strong support at 19,000 because of abundant liquidity in the territory.\par \par \uc2 The HSI has risen for nine sessions out of the past 11 for total gains of 19.54%.\par \par \uc2 "A bubble appears to be forming in the Hong Kong stock market, with too much cash chasing a limited supply of stocks," said Francis Lun, general manager at Fulbright Securities.\par \par \uc2 Continued capital inflows prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again Tuesday, injecting HK$3.1 billion to maintain the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Bullish sentiment continues to grow while valuations have gone well ahead of the expected improvements in economic activity and corporate earnings," Tai Fook Securities said.\par \par \uc2 Lun said Hong Kong shares will likely track any consolidation in Shanghai after the mainland's benchmark index has repeatedly hit 13-month highs. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index ended up 0.1% Tuesday at 3438.37, its highest close since June 2, 2008.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said Sichuan Expressway's 203% rise on its trading debut in Shanghai on Monday reflects the level of demand for new listings in China and bodes well for large Hong Kong-listed companies that hope to list on the mainland, including China Mobile and Cnooc.\par \par \uc2 China Mobile rose 4.0% to HK$83.60 and Cnooc was up 2.84% at HK$10.86.\par \par \uc2 Chinese steel makers were among the biggest gainers after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  upgraded the sector on stronger demand related to infrastructure spending and property investments in China, resulting in the country becoming a net importer of steel products from a net exporter.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  raised Angang Steel to buy from neutral and raised its target price to HK$20.00 from HK$12.90. Angang jumped 8.6% to a 13-month high of HK$16.96, and \uc2 Maanshan Iron & Steel\uc2  surged 7.9% to HK$5.84, the highest level since June 2, 2008.\par \par \uc2 Property developers were mostly higher on expectations abundant liquidity will continue to drive asset prices and support the local property market.\par \par \uc2 New World Development rose 4.4% to a 13-month high of HK$18.16. Wharf jumped 5.2% to HK$36.60, and Cheung Kong was up 1.4% at HK$102.80, its highest level since June last year.\par \par \uc2 Hang Seng Bank rose 4.5% to HK$122.10 after \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  raised the lender's price target to HK$135 from HK$105 on an improved 2009 earning outlook.\par \par \uc2 -By Jackie Cheung, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; jackie.cheung@dowjones.com [ 28-07-09 1035GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5013 55108   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090728e57s000l0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
