\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: HK Shares End At 10-Mo High Led By China Mobile\b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2009\par 11:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("HK Shares End At 10-Mo High Led By China Mobile; Above 20,000," published at 0845 GMT, misstated the period for the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the sixth paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Friday's gains on Wall Street and continued liquidity inflows led Hong Kong's benchmark index to close above 20,000 points Monday at a 10-month high, while heavyweight China Mobile caught up with the broader market's recent gains.\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Hang Seng Index rose 268.83 points, or 1.35%, to 20,251.62, its highest closing level since 20,491 on Sept. 9. It traded between 20,096.17 and 20,385.69 during the session.\par \par \uc2 Turnover totaled HK$80.90 billion, down from HK$83.64 billion Friday.\par \par \uc2 Traders said they expect the ample liquidity in the territory to continue to lend near-term support to the blue-chip index, which could test 20,500 this week.\par \par \uc2 "The momentum is still strong, fueled by strong liquidity inflows," said Peter Lai, a director at DBS Vickers Securities. "But I expect the Hang Seng Index to see more profit-taking pressure with the index facing strong resistance at 20,800-21,000."\par \par \uc2 The continued fund inflows prompted the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell a total HK$33.73 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since July 17 to maintain the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Local shares rose after a 0.26% gain in the Dow Jones Industrial Average on Friday. For the week, the DJIA was up 3.99%.\par \par \uc2 China Mobile led the blue-chip rally, rising 4.2% to HK$80.35 and contributing 76.06 points to the index's rise.\par \par \uc2 "China Mobile is lagging the recent market rally, and its valuation is becoming more attractive," said Eugene Law, research director at \uc2 Celestial Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Law said China Mobile is trading at a forward price/earnings ratio of about 12, while the average forward P/E of companies in the Hang Seng Index is 16-17.\par \par \uc2 "The stock is also trading at an unjustified discount versus peers \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  and \uc2 China Telecom\uc2 ," said Law, who set a price target of HK$92.00 for the stock.\par \par \uc2 Aluminum Corp. of China\uc2 , or Chalco, rose 4.5% to HK$9.22 though the company said it expects to swing to a net loss in the first half. Investors shrugged off the warning due to strengthening metals prices and bought the stock amid hopes of a turnaround in profitability in the second half, analysts said\par \par \uc2 ICEA said in a research note it expects the alumina and aluminum producer to return to a profit in the third quarter and recommended investors accumulate Chalco's Hong Kong-listed shares if they fall back to HK$6.98.\par \par \uc2 -By Lorraine Luk, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; lorraine.luk@dowjones.com [ 27-07-09 0934GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5013 55108   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090727e57r000h7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
